    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Prakasham_Barriage%2C_Vijayawada.jpg/1280px-Prakasham_Barriage%2C_Vijayawada.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "Resting on the banks of the mighty Krishna River, Vijayawada was born from divine conquest where the Mother Goddess defeated the demon army and Arjuna attained immortality.",
    "longDescription": "According to the Mahabharata, the third Pandava prince Arjuna performed rigorous penance atop the Indrakeeladri hill. Lord Shiva appeared before him disguised as a wild hunter (Kirata). After an epic duel that shook the mountain, Shiva revealed his cosmic form and gifted Arjuna the invincible weapon Pashupatastra. Later, Goddess Kanaka Durga established her golden shrine here after vanquishing the demon Mahishasura in an eight-day cosmic war.\n\nThe region flourished under the Vishnukundina kings in the 6th century, who carved the 4-tiered monolithic rock-cut cave temples of Undavalli featuring a massive 5-meter reclining Vishnu statue sculpted from single sandstone bedrock. Later Gajapati and Vijayanagara rulers fortified Kondapalli fort, renowned for soft Poniki wood toy artisans.\n\nVijayawada invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Andhra Pradesh.",
    "loreTitle": "The Citadel of Kanaka Durga & The Indrakeeladri Legend",
    "ancientLegend": "According to the Mahabharata, the third Pandava prince Arjuna performed rigorous penance atop the Indrakeeladri hill. Lord Shiva appeared before him disguised as a wild hunter (Kirata). After an epic duel that shook the mountain, Shiva revealed his cosmic form and gifted Arjuna the invincible weapon Pashupatastra. Later, Goddess Kanaka Durga established her golden shrine here after vanquishing the demon Mahishasura in an eight-day cosmic war.",
    "historicalChronicle": "The region flourished under the Vishnukundina kings in the 6th century, who carved the 4-tiered monolithic rock-cut cave temples of Undavalli featuring a massive 5-meter reclining Vishnu statue sculpted from single sandstone bedrock. Later Gajapati and Vijayanagara rulers fortified Kondapalli fort, renowned for soft Poniki wood toy artisans.",
