    "description": "Where lush Eastern Ghats plunge dramatically into the Bay of Bengal, Visakhapatnam is a realm of forgotten Buddhist monastic universities and seafaring maritime sagas.",
    "longDescription": "Mythology states that Visakhapatnam was named after a subterranean temple built by an Andhra king in honor of Lord Kartikeya (Visakha), which sank beneath the ocean waves. Local fisherfolk believe the deity still protects ships navigating through violent monsoon cyclones.\n\nOver two thousand years ago, cliffside monastic complexes like Thotlakonda and Bavikonda served as bustling Buddhist learning centers where monks meditated overlooking the ocean and welcomed trading ships sailing between Kalinga, Sri Lanka, and Southeast Asia.\n\nVisakhapatnam invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Andhra Pradesh.",
    "loreTitle": "The Mountain of the Dolphin & The Golden Stupas of Thotlakonda",
    "ancientLegend": "Mythology states that Visakhapatnam was named after a subterranean temple built by an Andhra king in honor of Lord Kartikeya (Visakha), which sank beneath the ocean waves. Local fisherfolk believe the deity still protects ships navigating through violent monsoon cyclones.",
