    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a7/Indian_Grand_Canyon_Sudhakar_Bichali.jpg/1280px-Indian_Grand_Canyon_Sudhakar_Bichali.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "A breathtaking sandstone chasm cut deep by the river Pennar, crowned by the impregnable 12th-century fort of the Pemmasani Nayakas.",
    "longDescription": "Telugu bards sing of the Gandikota gorge (Gandi meaning gorge, Kota meaning fort) as a divine cut sliced into the Erramala hills by the sword of Lord Narasimha to let the sacred Pennar river flow freely through the desert tableland.\n\nFounded in 1123 CE by Kapa Raja of nearby Bommanapalle, Gandikota was transformed into an unbreachable stronghold with 101 bastions by the Pemmasani Nayakas. It contains the magnificent Raghunatha and Madhavaraya temples with ornate granite pillars and the grand Jamia Masjid with two soaring minarets overlooking the gorge.\n\nGandikota invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Andhra Pradesh.",
    "loreTitle": "The Grand Canyon of Gandikota & The Citadel of the Red Gorge",
    "ancientLegend": "Telugu bards sing of the Gandikota gorge (Gandi meaning gorge, Kota meaning fort) as a divine cut sliced into the Erramala hills by the sword of Lord Narasimha to let the sacred Pennar river flow freely through the desert tableland.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Founded in 1123 CE by Kapa Raja of nearby Bommanapalle, Gandikota was transformed into an unbreachable stronghold with 101 bastions by the Pemmasani Nayakas. It contains the magnificent Raghunatha and Madhavaraya temples with ornate granite pillars and the grand Jamia Masjid with two soaring minarets overlooking the gorge.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Treasure of the 101 Bastions: Local legends whisper of royal gold and diamond hoards hidden inside hollowed sandstone boulders along the canyon cliff face.",
      "The Echo of the Nayaka Bugle: On windy dusk evenings, travelers claim to hear the brassy war-horn of the Pemmasani commanders whistling through the gorge crevices."
    ],
