    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Itafort_South_gate.png/1280px-Itafort_South_gate.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "Set against rainforest hills, Itanagar is an ancient tribal capital where medieval brick fortresses overlook mystical crater lakes shrouded in evergreen lore.",
    "longDescription": "The legendary Ita Fort was constructed using over eight million custom hand-fired terracotta bricks carried up steep rainforest ridges by thousands of devoted builders under King Ramachandra of the Chutiya dynasty. Deep within the surrounding forests lies Gyakar Sinyi (Ganga Lake), an emerald pool enclosed by primordial trees where tribal legends say celestial water nymphs protect the forest’s soul.\n\nBuilt during the 14th–15th century, Ita Fort’s irregular zigzag perimeter walls followed the natural contours of the ridge, featuring stone-carved entrance arches. The city stands as the cultural heartbeat of the Nyishi tribe, renowned for cane architecture and hornbill crest headdresses.\n\nItanagar invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Arunachal Pradesh.",
    "loreTitle": "The Legend of Ita Fort & The Emerald Spirit of Gyakar Sinyi",
    "ancientLegend": "The legendary Ita Fort was constructed using over eight million custom hand-fired terracotta bricks carried up steep rainforest ridges by thousands of devoted builders under King Ramachandra of the Chutiya dynasty. Deep within the surrounding forests lies Gyakar Sinyi (Ganga Lake), an emerald pool enclosed by primordial trees where tribal legends say celestial water nymphs protect the forest’s soul.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Built during the 14th–15th century, Ita Fort’s irregular zigzag perimeter walls followed the natural contours of the ridge, featuring stone-carved entrance arches. The city stands as the cultural heartbeat of the Nyishi tribe, renowned for cane architecture and hornbill crest headdresses.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Guardian Python of Ganga Lake: Locals believe a golden serpent guards the depths of Gyakar Sinyi and ensures the lake never dries up even during dry winters.",
      "The Singing Bricks: Villagers say that during thunderous monsoon nights, the ancient bricks of Ita Fort resonate with the war chants of King Ramachandra’s army."
    ],
