    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1e/A_cross_section_of_luch_green_valley_of_Ziro.jpg/1280px-A_cross_section_of_luch_green_valley_of_Ziro.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "A UNESCO World Heritage landscape of terraced paddy fields, pine-clad hillocks, and the ancient sustainable wisdom of the indigenous Apatani people.",
    "longDescription": "The Apatani people trace their lineage to their legendary ancestor Abotani, the first human created by the universe. To prevent women from being abducted by neighboring warrior tribes who were bewitched by their extraordinary beauty, the clan elders decreed that women would wear wooden nose plugs (Yaping Hullo) and vertical facial tattoos. The shamans (Nyibu) communicate with forest spirits through chants that have been preserved orally for over fifty generations.\n\nThe Apatani developed one of the world’s most sophisticated indigenous agro-ecosystems, cultivating wet rice integrated with fish farming in water channels without draft animals or modern machinery. Their unique sacred groves of blue pine and single-stem bamboo (Chimonobambusa callosa) have preserved ecological balance for centuries.\n\nZiro Valley invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Arunachal Pradesh.",
    "loreTitle": "The Covenant of Abotani & The Whispering Bamboo of Ziro",
    "ancientLegend": "The Apatani people trace their lineage to their legendary ancestor Abotani, the first human created by the universe. To prevent women from being abducted by neighboring warrior tribes who were bewitched by their extraordinary beauty, the clan elders decreed that women would wear wooden nose plugs (Yaping Hullo) and vertical facial tattoos. The shamans (Nyibu) communicate with forest spirits through chants that have been preserved orally for over fifty generations.",
    "historicalChronicle": "The Apatani developed one of the world’s most sophisticated indigenous agro-ecosystems, cultivating wet rice integrated with fish farming in water channels without draft animals or modern machinery. Their unique sacred groves of blue pine and single-stem bamboo (Chimonobambusa callosa) have preserved ecological balance for centuries.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Dree Festival Spirit Dance: During the agrarian Dree festival in July, village priests sacrifice fowl to appease Tamu and Metii spirits so pests spare the ripening paddies.",
      "The Sacred Totem of Myoko: Clan elders raise tall wooden poles (Babo) during the Myoko festival as spiritual antennas connecting earth to cosmic ancestors."
    ],
