    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Beauty_of_Kaziranga_National_Park.jpg/1280px-Beauty_of_Kaziranga_National_Park.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "A primordial floodplain of elephant grass, dense tropical forests, and sacred oxbow lakes harboring two-thirds of the world’s great Indian one-horned rhinoceroses.",
    "longDescription": "The name Kaziranga is rooted in the tragic romance of a young village boy named Kazi and a girl named Ranga. Forbidden by their families from being together, the star-crossed lovers disappeared into the dense wilderness. When search parties could find no trace of them, the forest elders realized the lovers had merged into the soul of the jungle, and the sacred wilderness was named Kaziranga in their honor.\n\nEstablished as a reserve forest in 1905 through the initiative of Mary Curzon after she failed to spot a single rhino, Kaziranga was shaped by annual Brahmaputra monsoonal floods that rejuvenate the nutrient-rich alluvial soil. It now supports the highest density of Royal Bengal tigers, wild water buffaloes, and Asiatic elephants.\n\nKaziranga invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Assam.",
    "loreTitle": "The Eternal Lovers of Kaziranga & The Rhino Guardian",
    "ancientLegend": "The name Kaziranga is rooted in the tragic romance of a young village boy named Kazi and a girl named Ranga. Forbidden by their families from being together, the star-crossed lovers disappeared into the dense wilderness. When search parties could find no trace of them, the forest elders realized the lovers had merged into the soul of the jungle, and the sacred wilderness was named Kaziranga in their honor.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Established as a reserve forest in 1905 through the initiative of Mary Curzon after she failed to spot a single rhino, Kaziranga was shaped by annual Brahmaputra monsoonal floods that rejuvenate the nutrient-rich alluvial soil. It now supports the highest density of Royal Bengal tigers, wild water buffaloes, and Asiatic elephants.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Ghost Herd of the Wetland: Forest guards on elephant patrols tell of seeing an ethereal phantom herd of white elephants stepping across the misty marshes before dawn.",
      "The Song of the Bihu Weavers: Women of the Karbi and Mishing tribes weave motifs of Kaziranga’s rhinos and wild orchids into their gamosas to bless hunters and travelers."
    ],
