    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6c/Ranghar_-_Assam.jpg/1280px-Ranghar_-_Assam.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "The capital of the mighty Ahom kingdom that ruled Assam for 600 years and undefeatedly repelled seventeen Mughal invasions with naval mastery.",
    "longDescription": "The Ahom kings, who traced their ancestry to the heavenly god Lengdon, built Sivasagar around vast man-made sacred lakes. Their royal amphitheatre, the Rang Ghar, is Asia’s oldest surviving royal sporting pavilion—shaped like an inverted Ahom war boat where kings watched elephant duels and buffalo fights while protected by sacred dragon banners.\n\nBuilt during the reign of Swargadeo Rudra Singha and Pramatta Singha in the 18th century, the monuments include the 7-storey Talatal Ghar with underground escape tunnels running to the Dikhow River, and the 104-foot Sivadol temple standing on the banks of the massive 257-acre Sivasagar tank.\n\nSivasagar invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Assam.",
    "loreTitle": "The Citadel of the Ahom Kings & The Rang Ghar Dragon Roof",
    "ancientLegend": "The Ahom kings, who traced their ancestry to the heavenly god Lengdon, built Sivasagar around vast man-made sacred lakes. Their royal amphitheatre, the Rang Ghar, is Asia’s oldest surviving royal sporting pavilion—shaped like an inverted Ahom war boat where kings watched elephant duels and buffalo fights while protected by sacred dragon banners.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Built during the reign of Swargadeo Rudra Singha and Pramatta Singha in the 18th century, the monuments include the 7-storey Talatal Ghar with underground escape tunnels running to the Dikhow River, and the 104-foot Sivadol temple standing on the banks of the massive 257-acre Sivasagar tank.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Secret Tunnels of Talatal Ghar: Local legend says the maze of lower subterranean tunnels under the palace was sealed after British soldiers ventured in and never returned.",
      "The Golden Kalash of Sivadol: High atop the temple dome sits an 8-foot pure gold kalash that was struck by lightning in ancient times yet remained entirely unblemished."
    ],
