    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Chitrakot_waterfalls.JPG/1280px-Chitrakot_waterfalls.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "The cultural capital of Bastar, where indigenous Maria and Muria tribes have preserved 4,000-year-old Dhokra bell-metal casting and the 75-day long Bastar Dussehra festival dedicated to Goddess Danteshwari.",
    "longDescription": "Tribal lore speaks of Dandakaranya—the primordial jungle where Lord Rama spent much of his fourteen-year exile. Tribal elders recount that King Purushottam Dev of Kakatiya lineage undertook a pilgrimage on foot to Puri, and the Gajapati King bestowed upon him the sacred title of Rathapati, gifting him the tradition of the 16-wheeled wooden chariot pulled solely by forest tribes.\n\nRuled by the Kakatiya rulers who migrated from Warangal in the 14th century, Jagdalpur developed into an oasis of tribal art. Its craftsmen still use bees-wax coils, river silt, and molten brass to forge iconic Dhokra artifacts without any molds, ensuring every creation is completely unique.\n\nJagdalpur invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Chhattisgarh.",
    "loreTitle": "The Bastar Dussehra of the Forest Kings & The Bell Metal Alchemists",
    "ancientLegend": "Tribal lore speaks of Dandakaranya—the primordial jungle where Lord Rama spent much of his fourteen-year exile. Tribal elders recount that King Purushottam Dev of Kakatiya lineage undertook a pilgrimage on foot to Puri, and the Gajapati King bestowed upon him the sacred title of Rathapati, gifting him the tradition of the 16-wheeled wooden chariot pulled solely by forest tribes.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Ruled by the Kakatiya rulers who migrated from Warangal in the 14th century, Jagdalpur developed into an oasis of tribal art. Its craftsmen still use bees-wax coils, river silt, and molten brass to forge iconic Dhokra artifacts without any molds, ensuring every creation is completely unique.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The 75-Day Dussehra Without Effigies: Unlike the rest of India, Bastar Dussehra does not celebrate the slaying of Ravana; instead, it is a magnificent 75-day council where forest deities convene to bless the tribal lands.",
      "The Bison Horn Headdress: Maria dancers wear majestic wild bison horn crowns adorned with cowrie shells, believing it channels the untamed vitality of the forest guardian spirit."
    ],
