    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Front_Elevation_of_Basilica_of_Bom_Jesus.jpg/1280px-Front_Elevation_of_Basilica_of_Bom_Jesus.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "Once the magnificent maritime capital of the Portuguese Empire in Asia, Old Goa houses colossal Baroque cathedrals and the miracle shrine of the Basilica of Bom Jesus.",
    "longDescription": "Known in the 16th century as Goa Dourada (\"Golden Goa\"), the city was so opulent that travelers claimed anyone who saw Goa needed no longer look upon Lisbon. The Basilica of Bom Jesus holds the sacred silver casket containing the incorrupt body of St. Francis Xavier, which has remained miraculously preserved without embalming for nearly five centuries.\n\nBuilt during the zenith of Portuguese naval power, Se Cathedral features the famous \"Golden Bell\"—the largest church bell in Asia whose rich resonant tone could be heard across the river Mandovi. Abandoned after devastating cholera and malaria epidemics in the 18th century, Old Goa remains a UNESCO World Heritage sanctuary of Portuguese-Manueline architecture.\n\nOld Goa invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Goa.",
    "loreTitle": "The Incorrupt Body of St. Francis Xavier & The Golden Bells of Se",
    "ancientLegend": "Known in the 16th century as Goa Dourada (\"Golden Goa\"), the city was so opulent that travelers claimed anyone who saw Goa needed no longer look upon Lisbon. The Basilica of Bom Jesus holds the sacred silver casket containing the incorrupt body of St. Francis Xavier, which has remained miraculously preserved without embalming for nearly five centuries.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Built during the zenith of Portuguese naval power, Se Cathedral features the famous \"Golden Bell\"—the largest church bell in Asia whose rich resonant tone could be heard across the river Mandovi. Abandoned after devastating cholera and malaria epidemics in the 18th century, Old Goa remains a UNESCO World Heritage sanctuary of Portuguese-Manueline architecture.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Miraculous Growing Cross: Inside the Church of St. Francis of Assisi hangs a simple wooden cross discovered in 1619 that is said to have miraculously grown in height over the centuries.",
      "The Secret River Crypt: Local boatmen whisper of subterranean vaults connecting the Viceroy’s Arch to submerged galleon dockyards along the Mandovi riverbed."
    ],
