    "id": "loc_0029",
    "name": "Champaner",
    "tagline": "The hill fortress of the dancing goddess and forgotten Sultanate arches",
    "state": "Gujarat",
    "region": "Western India",
    "category": "Heritage",
    "secondaryCategories": [
      "Culture",
      "Nature"
    ],
    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/86/Jain_Temple%2C_Pavagadh_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled",
    "description": "A UNESCO World Heritage marvel where Hindu-Jain cliff shrines atop volcanic Pavagadh hill blend with the pristine 15th-century sandstone mosques of Sultan Mahmud Begada below.",
    "longDescription": "According to the Devi Bhagavata Purana, Pavagadh hill represents the right toe of Goddess Sati which fell here during Shiva’s cosmic sorrow. The ancient temple of Kalika Mata crowns the highest cliff, reached by climbing 2,000 steps or soaring above the rainforest canopy by cable car.\n\nCaptured in 1484 by Mahmud Begada, Champaner served as Gujarat’s capital for decades. Its Jama Masjid is acclaimed as one of India’s finest Indo-Islamic monuments, featuring 172 carved pillars, soaring octagonal domes, and sun-filtering jali screens designed to catch cool breezes off the volcanic ridge.\n\nChampaner invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Gujarat.",
    "loreTitle": "The Ropeway of Kalika Mata & The Golden Citadel of Champaner",
    "ancientLegend": "According to the Devi Bhagavata Purana, Pavagadh hill represents the right toe of Goddess Sati which fell here during Shiva’s cosmic sorrow. The ancient temple of Kalika Mata crowns the highest cliff, reached by climbing 2,000 steps or soaring above the rainforest canopy by cable car.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Captured in 1484 by Mahmud Begada, Champaner served as Gujarat’s capital for decades. Its Jama Masjid is acclaimed as one of India’s finest Indo-Islamic monuments, featuring 172 carved pillars, soaring octagonal domes, and sun-filtering jali screens designed to catch cool breezes off the volcanic ridge.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Midnight Dance of Goddess Kali: Bards sing of King Patai Raval who visited the temple during Navratri and foolishly stared with lust at a celestial dancer, who was the Goddess herself in disguise. Enraged, she cursed his kingdom to fall to ruin within a year.",
      "The Subterranean Water Palace: The intricate Helical Stepwell of Champaner is built in a spiral stone staircase descending 16 meters into the earth, aligned so sunlight illuminates the water table only at true solar noon."
    ],
