    "description": "The only sanctuary on planet Earth where the Asiatic lion roams in the wild, coexisting with indigenous Maldhari pastoralists in the rugged scrub forests of Gir.",
    "longDescription": "Saurashtra folklore considers the Asiatic lion (Sinh) to be the vahana (mount) of Goddess Durga. For centuries, the nomadic Maldhari tribes have lived inside the deep forest in circular mud homesteads called Nesses, grazing their Gir cattle alongside prides of lions in an unspoken ancient covenant of mutual respect.\n\nBy the early 20th century, excessive hunting had reduced the Asiatic lion population to less than 20 individuals. The Nawab of Junagadh, Muhammad Rasul Khanji, recognized the impending catastrophe and declared Gir a strictly protected reserve in 1900, single-handedly saving the species from global extinction.\n\nGir invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Gujarat.",
    "loreTitle": "The Maldhari Herdsmen & The Royal Guardians of the Forest",
    "ancientLegend": "Saurashtra folklore considers the Asiatic lion (Sinh) to be the vahana (mount) of Goddess Durga. For centuries, the nomadic Maldhari tribes have lived inside the deep forest in circular mud homesteads called Nesses, grazing their Gir cattle alongside prides of lions in an unspoken ancient covenant of mutual respect.",
    "historicalChronicle": "By the early 20th century, excessive hunting had reduced the Asiatic lion population to less than 20 individuals. The Nawab of Junagadh, Muhammad Rasul Khanji, recognized the impending catastrophe and declared Gir a strictly protected reserve in 1900, single-handedly saving the species from global extinction.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Maldhari Call: Maldhari herdsmen have a unique rhythmic call that warns lions of their approach through the thorny brush, preventing sudden confrontations.",
      "The Crocodile River of Kamleshwar: The Kamleshwar Dam reservoir in the center of Gir is home to hundreds of massive marsh crocodiles, said to have been fed by forest hermits since medieval times."
    ],
