    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Tomb_of_Mahabat_Khan.jpg/1280px-Tomb_of_Mahabat_Khan.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "A historical jewel at the foot of sacred Mount Girnar, famous for 2,300-year-old Ashokan rock edicts, the unbreachable Uparkot Fort, and the surreal Mahabat Maqbara mausoleum.",
    "longDescription": "Mount Girnar is older than the Himalayas according to Jain and Hindu lore. It was here that the 22nd Jain Tirthankara Neminatha attained liberation, and Lord Dattatreya performed penance atop the highest pinnacle. Every year during Mahashivratri, thousands of naked Naga Sadhus emerge from hidden Himalayan caves to march in torchlit procession to the Bhavnath temple.\n\nJunagadh (\"Old Fort\") features the 3rd-century BCE Rock Edicts of Emperor Ashoka inscribed in Brahmi script on a massive granite boulder. Uparkot Fort survived 16 separate sieges over 800 years, relying on its deep rock-cut stepwells Adi Kadi Vav and Navghan Kuvo which were carved directly into solid stone.\n\nJunagadh invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Gujarat.",
    "loreTitle": "The Citadel of Uparkot & The Ascetics of Mount Girnar",
    "ancientLegend": "Mount Girnar is older than the Himalayas according to Jain and Hindu lore. It was here that the 22nd Jain Tirthankara Neminatha attained liberation, and Lord Dattatreya performed penance atop the highest pinnacle. Every year during Mahashivratri, thousands of naked Naga Sadhus emerge from hidden Himalayan caves to march in torchlit procession to the Bhavnath temple.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Junagadh (\"Old Fort\") features the 3rd-century BCE Rock Edicts of Emperor Ashoka inscribed in Brahmi script on a massive granite boulder. Uparkot Fort survived 16 separate sieges over 800 years, relying on its deep rock-cut stepwells Adi Kadi Vav and Navghan Kuvo which were carved directly into solid stone.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Vanishing Naga Sadhus of Bhavnath: Legend says that during the midnight plunge into the holy Mrigi Kund during Mahashivratri, several realized Naga babas enter the water and never resurface, merging directly into the spiritual ether.",
      "The Ghost Princess of Uparkot: Folk ballads celebrate Queen Ranakdevi, who threw herself into the pyre after King Ra Khengar was defeated, cursing the river Bhogavo to flow dry forever."
    ],
