    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/The_White_Desert_in_Kutch%2C_the_great_rann_of_kutch.jpg/1280px-The_White_Desert_in_Kutch%2C_the_great_rann_of_kutch.jpg",
    "description": "The world’s largest salt desert, where the Great Rann transforms into a shimmering silver mirror on full moon nights, alive with Kutchi folk melodies, Rogan art, and Rabari embroidery.",
    "longDescription": "Local folklore speaks of the holy saint Dhrangad Nath who performed rigorous penance atop Kalo Dungar (Black Hill). When wild jackals gathered around him starving, the compassionate saint offered his own body, crying \"Le Ang\" (Take my limb). To this day, temple priests at Kalo Dungar offer food to wild jackals after the evening aarti, and the beasts arrive peacefully from the desert to feed.\n\nKutch was once an open arm of the Arabian Sea until massive earthquakes over millennia and the Indus river diversion created the vast seasonal salt flats. The traditional mud houses (Bhunga) with mirror-work clay art (Lippan Kaam) survived the devastating 2001 earthquake unharmed due to their circular aerodynamic design.\n\nKutch invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Gujarat.",
    "loreTitle": "The Mirage of the Rann & The Embroidery of the Nomads",
    "ancientLegend": "Local folklore speaks of the holy saint Dhrangad Nath who performed rigorous penance atop Kalo Dungar (Black Hill). When wild jackals gathered around him starving, the compassionate saint offered his own body, crying \"Le Ang\" (Take my limb). To this day, temple priests at Kalo Dungar offer food to wild jackals after the evening aarti, and the beasts arrive peacefully from the desert to feed.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Kutch was once an open arm of the Arabian Sea until massive earthquakes over millennia and the Indus river diversion created the vast seasonal salt flats. The traditional mud houses (Bhunga) with mirror-work clay art (Lippan Kaam) survived the devastating 2001 earthquake unharmed due to their circular aerodynamic design.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Chir Batti Ghost Lights: Across the marshy Banni grasslands on dark nights, locals observe mysterious dancing orbs of light known as Chir Batti (Ghost Lights) that follow travelers and vanish without a trace.",
      "The Secret Rogan Tree Oil: In the tiny village of Nirona, the Khatri family preserves the 400-year-old Persian Rogan art, using boiled castor seed oil and natural mineral pigments worked with a metal stylus to weave floating silk-like patterns."
    ],
