    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Somanath_mandir_%28cropped%29.jpg/1280px-Somanath_mandir_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "Standing on the shore of the roaring Arabian Sea, Somnath is the foremost among the twelve holy Jyotirlingas, symbolizing the immortal triumph of faith over centuries of destruction.",
    "longDescription": "According to the Skanda Purana, the Moon God (Soma) lost his radiant glow due to a curse from King Daksha. Soma bathed at the confluence of the sacred Saraswati, Hiran, and Kapila rivers (Triveni Sangam) and worshipped Lord Shiva, who restored his luminous waxing and waning cycle. In gratitude, Soma built the first golden temple here.\n\nRebuilt repeatedly after seventeen major invasions and sackings across a thousand years, Somnath stands as an architectural emblem of resilience. The present temple in Chalukya stone architecture was consecrated in 1951 through the leadership of Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel.\n\nSomnath invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Gujarat.",
    "loreTitle": "The Shrine of Chandra Dev & The Arrow of the Unbroken Ocean",
    "ancientLegend": "According to the Skanda Purana, the Moon God (Soma) lost his radiant glow due to a curse from King Daksha. Soma bathed at the confluence of the sacred Saraswati, Hiran, and Kapila rivers (Triveni Sangam) and worshipped Lord Shiva, who restored his luminous waxing and waning cycle. In gratitude, Soma built the first golden temple here.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Rebuilt repeatedly after seventeen major invasions and sackings across a thousand years, Somnath stands as an architectural emblem of resilience. The present temple in Chalukya stone architecture was consecrated in 1951 through the leadership of Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Baan Stambh (Arrow Pillar): On the sea-facing wall stands the ancient Baan Stambh pillar pointing south with an inscription stating that there is no obstruction of land from this point all the way to the South Pole (Antarctica).",
      "The Floating Shivling of 1026: Ancient travelers recorded that the original sanctum contained a magnetic lodestone shivling that hung suspended in midair without any visible physical support."
    ],
