    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Lakshmi_Vilas_Palace%2C_Vadodara.jpg/1280px-Lakshmi_Vilas_Palace%2C_Vadodara.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "The majestic cultural capital of Gujarat, celebrated for the palatial Laxmi Vilas Palace—four times the size of Buckingham Palace—and the world’s largest private collection of Raja Ravi Varma paintings.",
    "longDescription": "The city was anciently known as Vadapadraka (\"The Village Amongst Banyan Trees\"). Bards sing of Sage Vishwamitra who meditated along the river Vishwamitri, whose sacred waters were guarded by divine marsh crocodiles who protected the sage’s hermitage from marauding demons.\n\nUnder the visionary rule of Maharaja Sayajirao Gaekwad III in the late 19th and early 20th century, Baroda became a pioneer in free primary education, public libraries, and the arts. Built in 1890 in Indo-Saracenic style with Venetian mosaics and Belgian stained glass, Laxmi Vilas Palace remains the residence of the Gaekwad royal family.\n\nVadodara invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Gujarat.",
    "loreTitle": "The Grandeur of Laxmi Vilas & The Canvas of Raja Ravi Varma",
    "ancientLegend": "The city was anciently known as Vadapadraka (\"The Village Amongst Banyan Trees\"). Bards sing of Sage Vishwamitra who meditated along the river Vishwamitri, whose sacred waters were guarded by divine marsh crocodiles who protected the sage’s hermitage from marauding demons.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Under the visionary rule of Maharaja Sayajirao Gaekwad III in the late 19th and early 20th century, Baroda became a pioneer in free primary education, public libraries, and the arts. Built in 1890 in Indo-Saracenic style with Venetian mosaics and Belgian stained glass, Laxmi Vilas Palace remains the residence of the Gaekwad royal family.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Maharaja’s Toy Train: Sayajirao III had a miniature steam train built specifically to carry the royal children around the 500-acre palace grounds between the mango orchards and the deer park.",
      "The Crocodile of the Palace Moat: Local folklore remembers the royal menagerie where prized crocodiles in the Vishwamitri were fed raw meat to keep the palace fortifications secure."
    ],
