    "id": "loc_0049",
    "name": "Dholavira",
    "tagline": "The 5,000-year-old Harappan metropolis of geometric stone reservoirs",
    "state": "Gujarat",
    "region": "Western India",
    "category": "Heritage",
    "secondaryCategories": [
      "Culture",
      "Nature"
    ],
    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/DHOLAVIRA_SITE_%2824%29.jpg/1280px-DHOLAVIRA_SITE_%2824%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "A colossal Indus Valley Civilization city on Khadir Bet island in the Great Rann of Kutch, featuring the ancient world’s most advanced rainwater harvesting engineering and stone masonry.",
    "longDescription": "Desert nomads long believed the mound of Kotada Timba was the enchanted city of ancient demigods who could summon monsoons into underground stone cisterns. When archaeologists excavated the site in 1990, they unearthed a planned Bronze Age city spanning over 100 hectares with monumental stone gateways, bead-making workshops, and tiered reservoirs.\n\nOccupied from 2650 BCE to 1450 BCE, Dholavira is unique among Harappan sites for its extensive use of dressed stone instead of sun-dried mud bricks. Its master engineers channeled seasonal streams into sixteen massive rock-cut reservoirs that could store 300,000 cubic meters of water, sustaining thousands of inhabitants in an arid desert climate.\n\nDholavira invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Gujarat.",
    "loreTitle": "The Citadel of the White Desert & The Oldest Signboard in the World",
    "ancientLegend": "Desert nomads long believed the mound of Kotada Timba was the enchanted city of ancient demigods who could summon monsoons into underground stone cisterns. When archaeologists excavated the site in 1990, they unearthed a planned Bronze Age city spanning over 100 hectares with monumental stone gateways, bead-making workshops, and tiered reservoirs.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Occupied from 2650 BCE to 1450 BCE, Dholavira is unique among Harappan sites for its extensive use of dressed stone instead of sun-dried mud bricks. Its master engineers channeled seasonal streams into sixteen massive rock-cut reservoirs that could store 300,000 cubic meters of water, sustaining thousands of inhabitants in an arid desert climate.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Ten-Letter Wooden Signboard: Near the northern gateway of the citadel, archaeologists found ten giant Indus script symbols crafted from crystalline gypsum inlaid into a wooden board, considered humanity’s oldest surviving civic signboard.",
      "The Polished Stone Pillars: The stone columns found at Dholavira exhibit a mirror-like polish that was previously thought to have been invented two thousand years later during the Mauryan Empire."
    ],
