    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a2/Kasol_mountain_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled",
    "description": "Nestled along the emerald Parvati River amidst towering deodar pines, Kasol is a bohemian mountain paradise rooted in profound mythological hot springs and ancient riverside folklore.",
    "longDescription": "Legend recounts that Lord Shiva and Goddess Parvati meditated in this valley for over a thousand years. While bathing in the river, Parvati lost her celestial gem (Mani) in the waters. Shiva commanded his attendants to search for it, but when they failed, Shiva opened his third eye in cosmic fury. The serpent king Sheshanaga hissed out the gem from the depths of the earth along with boiling water springs to appease the Lord.\n\nKasol has long served as the base for high-altitude treks into Kheerganga, Tosh, and the mysterious secluded village of Malana—whose inhabitants claim direct ancestry to the Greek soldiers of Alexander the Great and speak an isolated Sino-Tibetan language called Kanashi.\n\nKasol invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Himachal Pradesh.",
    "loreTitle": "The Boiling Springs of Manikaran & The Jewel of Goddess Parvati",
    "ancientLegend": "Legend recounts that Lord Shiva and Goddess Parvati meditated in this valley for over a thousand years. While bathing in the river, Parvati lost her celestial gem (Mani) in the waters. Shiva commanded his attendants to search for it, but when they failed, Shiva opened his third eye in cosmic fury. The serpent king Sheshanaga hissed out the gem from the depths of the earth along with boiling water springs to appease the Lord.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Kasol has long served as the base for high-altitude treks into Kheerganga, Tosh, and the mysterious secluded village of Malana—whose inhabitants claim direct ancestry to the Greek soldiers of Alexander the Great and speak an isolated Sino-Tibetan language called Kanashi.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Rice Cooked in Mountain Springs: At the sacred Gurdwara of Manikaran, raw rice, lentils, and flatbreads are cooked inside submerged linen bags directly in boiling natural sulphur springs without any fire.",
      "The Sacred Law of Jamlu Devta in Malana: In nearby Malana, outsiders are forbidden from touching the villagers or their stone walls, as the village is ruled exclusively by the ancient decree of their deity Jamlu Devta."
    ],
