    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/66/Kinnaur_kailash%286050m%29_and_Jorkanden_%286473_m%29_from_Kalpa_photographed_By_Sumita_Roy.jpg/1280px-Kinnaur_kailash%286050m%29_and_Jorkanden_%286473_m%29_from_Kalpa_photographed_By_Sumita_Roy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "A dramatic borderland of vertical gorge roads, slate-roofed temples, and ancient Hindu-Buddhist traditions where mythical celestial musicians once sang for the gods.",
    "longDescription": "In Indian mythology, the Kinnaras were demigods possessing supernatural musical voices and celestial beauty, who lived in the cloud-covered cliffs between heaven and earth. Here stands the sacred 79-foot natural rock monolith of Kinnaur Kailash (19,850 ft), which is believed to miraculously change colors throughout the day from charcoal grey to fiery crimson at sunset.\n\nCut directly into sheer granite cliffs along the Hindustan-Tibet road, Kinnaur preserves timber-bonded architecture like the Kamru Fort in Sangla Valley, where rulers of the Bushahr kingdom were crowned for over a hundred generations.\n\nKinnaur invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Himachal Pradesh.",
    "loreTitle": "The Land of the Kinnaras & The Changing Monolith of Kailash",
    "ancientLegend": "In Indian mythology, the Kinnaras were demigods possessing supernatural musical voices and celestial beauty, who lived in the cloud-covered cliffs between heaven and earth. Here stands the sacred 79-foot natural rock monolith of Kinnaur Kailash (19,850 ft), which is believed to miraculously change colors throughout the day from charcoal grey to fiery crimson at sunset.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Cut directly into sheer granite cliffs along the Hindustan-Tibet road, Kinnaur preserves timber-bonded architecture like the Kamru Fort in Sangla Valley, where rulers of the Bushahr kingdom were crowned for over a hundred generations.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Changing Color of the Shivalinga: Pilgrims undertaking the arduous parikrama witness the Kinnaur Kailash rock pillar shift from dark grey in morning mist to amber gold at noon and glowing red at twilight.",
      "The Ancient Wooden Gods of Kalpa: In the village of Kalpa, wooden village deities (Devtas) are carried on wooden palanquins by villagers, and communicate their will through sudden swinging motions interpreted by the village shaman."
    ],
