    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/LiddarRiverJhelum.jpeg/1280px-LiddarRiverJhelum.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "Where the gushing Lidder River cuts through dense pine valleys, Pahalgam is the traditional staging ground for the sacred Amarnath Yatra and the gateway to Betaab and Aru valleys.",
    "longDescription": "Mythology states that when Lord Shiva was traveling to the Amarnath Cave to reveal the secret of eternal life (Amar Katha) to Goddess Parvati, he left behind all his worldly companions one by one: his bull Nandi at Pahalgam (Bailgaon), the moon from his locks at Chandanwari, his snake Vasuki at Sheshnag Lake, and his son Ganesha at Mahagunas Parvat.\n\nPahalgam has been the ancestral pasture of the Gujjar and Bakarwal nomad shepherds for centuries. The nearby 8th-century stone temple ruins of Martand Sun Temple, built by King Lalitaditya Muktapida, stand as the crowning masterwork of classical Kashmiri stone architecture.\n\nPahalgam invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Jammu and Kashmir.",
    "loreTitle": "The Valley of Shepherds & The Cave of the Secret of Immortality",
    "ancientLegend": "Mythology states that when Lord Shiva was traveling to the Amarnath Cave to reveal the secret of eternal life (Amar Katha) to Goddess Parvati, he left behind all his worldly companions one by one: his bull Nandi at Pahalgam (Bailgaon), the moon from his locks at Chandanwari, his snake Vasuki at Sheshnag Lake, and his son Ganesha at Mahagunas Parvat.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Pahalgam has been the ancestral pasture of the Gujjar and Bakarwal nomad shepherds for centuries. The nearby 8th-century stone temple ruins of Martand Sun Temple, built by King Lalitaditya Muktapida, stand as the crowning masterwork of classical Kashmiri stone architecture.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Immortal Pigeons of Amarnath: Legend says a pair of white pigeons overheard Lord Shiva reciting the Amar Katha inside the cave and became immortal; pilgrims climbing from Pahalgam still report spotting two white doves fluttering near the icy cave entrance.",
      "The Emerald Depth of Sheshnag: The high-altitude Sheshnag Lake is named after the mythical cosmic serpent, whose seven heads are believed to form the jagged mountain peaks framing the lake."
    ],
