    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a2/Sonmarg_rishav7336.jpg/1280px-Sonmarg_rishav7336.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "A dramatic alpine gateway at 9,000 feet where the roaring Sindh River originates from melting glaciers, surrounded by birch forests and towering snow pinnacles.",
    "longDescription": "Ancient Kashmiri bards called the valley Sonamarg (\"Meadow of Gold\") because during spring and summer, the entire valley floor is carpeted with golden alpine buttercups and kingcups, and the surrounding glacier waters were believed to carry flakes of cosmic gold from Mount Meru.\n\nHistorically, Sonamarg was the critical mountain gateway on the ancient Silk Route connecting Kashmir to Ladakh, Tibet, and Central Asia. The Thajiwas Glacier descends right to the valley floor, providing a living laboratory of glacial moraines and pristine pine trails.\n\nSonamarg invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Jammu and Kashmir.",
    "loreTitle": "The Meadow of Gold & The Thajiwas Glacier Ice Bridges",
    "ancientLegend": "Ancient Kashmiri bards called the valley Sonamarg (\"Meadow of Gold\") because during spring and summer, the entire valley floor is carpeted with golden alpine buttercups and kingcups, and the surrounding glacier waters were believed to carry flakes of cosmic gold from Mount Meru.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Historically, Sonamarg was the critical mountain gateway on the ancient Silk Route connecting Kashmir to Ladakh, Tibet, and Central Asia. The Thajiwas Glacier descends right to the valley floor, providing a living laboratory of glacial moraines and pristine pine trails.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Seven Alpine Lakes: The high mountain passes behind Sonamarg lead to the legendary Great Lakes of Kashmir (Vishansar, Kishansar, Gadsar), where local shepherds believe water fairies guard underwater jewel palaces.",
      "The Golden Trout of Sindh: Anglers say the swift glacier-fed waters of the Sindh river contain silver and golden trout blessed by mountain hermits to be faster than lightning."
    ],
