    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Dal_Lake_Hazratbal_Srinagar.jpg/1280px-Dal_Lake_Hazratbal_Srinagar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "The jewel of the Kashmir Valley, celebrated for its floating vegetable markets on Dal Lake, hand-carved cedar houseboats, and terraced Mughal gardens stepping down from the Zabarwan mountains.",
    "longDescription": "According to the ancient Sanskrit chronicle Rajatarangini by Kalhana, the entire Kashmir Valley was originally a vast inland lake called Satisar (\"The Lake of Sati\"). Sage Kashyapa drained the waters through a gap in the Baramulla hills with the stroke of his trident to destroy the water demon Jalodbhava, creating a paradise for humanity.\n\nMughal Emperor Jahangir fell so deeply in love with Srinagar that on his deathbed he famously whispered: \"Kashmir, only Kashmir.\" Emperors built the Nishat Bagh, Shalimar Bagh, and Chashme Shahi terraced fountains. The white marble Hazratbal Shrine houses the sacred Moi-e-Muqqadas (relic hair of Prophet Muhammad).\n\nSrinagar invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Jammu and Kashmir.",
    "loreTitle": "The Chinar Trees of Dal Lake & The Miracle of Hazratbal",
    "ancientLegend": "According to the ancient Sanskrit chronicle Rajatarangini by Kalhana, the entire Kashmir Valley was originally a vast inland lake called Satisar (\"The Lake of Sati\"). Sage Kashyapa drained the waters through a gap in the Baramulla hills with the stroke of his trident to destroy the water demon Jalodbhava, creating a paradise for humanity.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Mughal Emperor Jahangir fell so deeply in love with Srinagar that on his deathbed he famously whispered: \"Kashmir, only Kashmir.\" Emperors built the Nishat Bagh, Shalimar Bagh, and Chashme Shahi terraced fountains. The white marble Hazratbal Shrine houses the sacred Moi-e-Muqqadas (relic hair of Prophet Muhammad).",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Floating Garden Mystery: Farmers on Dal Lake build floating rafts of dried reeds and silt called \"Radh\", where tomatoes and lotus roots grow directly on water and can literally be towed away by boat.",
      "The Whispering Chinar of Char Chinar: In the middle of Dal Lake sits an islet with four ancient Chinar trees aligned to the four cardinal directions; Mughal nobles believed sitting in their center granted everlasting inner peace."
    ],
