    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/ce/Pine_trees_of_Netarhat_Hill_station.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled",
    "description": "Perched at 3,600 feet on the Chotanagpur plateau, Netarhat is a peaceful hill haven of dense sal forests, pear orchards, and dramatic sunrise and sunset vistas.",
    "longDescription": "Local Asur and Birhor tribal legends describe the Netarhat plateau (\"The Bamboo Market\") as a sacred forest tableland where ancestral spirit deities guard the mountain passes from wild storms. The dense jungle is crisscrossed by crystalline streams like Upper and Lower Ghaghri waterfalls.\n\nDuring the colonial era, Netarhat was developed as a secluded sanatorium for British officials seeking respite from the summer heat of the plains. Netarhat Residential School, established in 1954, became one of India’s most celebrated educational institutions producing premier scientists and civil servants.\n\nNetarhat invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Jharkhand.",
    "loreTitle": "The Magnolia Sunset Point & The Legend of the British Maiden",
    "ancientLegend": "Local Asur and Birhor tribal legends describe the Netarhat plateau (\"The Bamboo Market\") as a sacred forest tableland where ancestral spirit deities guard the mountain passes from wild storms. The dense jungle is crisscrossed by crystalline streams like Upper and Lower Ghaghri waterfalls.",
    "historicalChronicle": "During the colonial era, Netarhat was developed as a secluded sanatorium for British officials seeking respite from the summer heat of the plains. Netarhat Residential School, established in 1954, became one of India’s most celebrated educational institutions producing premier scientists and civil servants.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Tragedy of Magnolia Point: Local folklore tells of a British maiden named Magnolia who fell in love with a local tribal shepherd boy; when their love was forbidden by colonial society, she rode her horse off the edge of the cliff into the abyss, giving the sunset point its romantic name.",
      "The Lodh Falls Dragon Roar: The 468-foot Lodh Falls (Jharkhand’s highest waterfall) is said to roar like a sleeping dragon when the first monsoons strike the Chotanagpur ridge."
    ],
