    "id": "loc_0050",
    "name": "Aihole",
    "tagline": "The cradle of Indian temple architecture where stone temples took form",
    "state": "Karnataka",
    "region": "Southern India",
    "category": "Heritage",
    "secondaryCategories": [
      "Culture",
      "Nature"
    ],
    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9d/Durga_Temple_-_General_View.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled",
    "description": "Nestled on the banks of the Malaprabha River, Aihole is an open-air museum of over 120 stone temples built between the 5th and 8th centuries by master Chalukyan guild sculptors.",
    "longDescription": "Mythology states that after Lord Parashurama avenged his father’s death by defeating tyrannical kings, he washed his bloody axe in the Malaprabha River. Seeing the river water turn blood-red, a woman cried \"Ayyo, Hole!\" (\"Ah, the river!\"), giving the town its ancient name.\n\nAihole served as the architectural workshop where Chalukyan artisans experimented with rock-cut caves, apsidal sanctums, and tiered spires. The iconic 7th-century Durga Temple features a unique semi-circular Buddhist chaitya-inspired apsidal design with exquisitely carved ceiling panels of celestial couples and fierce guardian deities.\n\nAihole invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Karnataka.",
    "loreTitle": "The Laboratory of Chalukyan Architects & The Durga Temple Curve",
    "ancientLegend": "Mythology states that after Lord Parashurama avenged his father’s death by defeating tyrannical kings, he washed his bloody axe in the Malaprabha River. Seeing the river water turn blood-red, a woman cried \"Ayyo, Hole!\" (\"Ah, the river!\"), giving the town its ancient name.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Aihole served as the architectural workshop where Chalukyan artisans experimented with rock-cut caves, apsidal sanctums, and tiered spires. The iconic 7th-century Durga Temple features a unique semi-circular Buddhist chaitya-inspired apsidal design with exquisitely carved ceiling panels of celestial couples and fierce guardian deities.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Guild of 500 Merchants: Inscriptions at Aihole celebrate the \"Ayyavole 500\", an ancient medieval trading guild whose mercantile ships traveled as far as Sumatra, Persia, and China.",
      "The Meguti Hill Inscription: High atop Meguti Hill, the Jain temple contains the famous 634 CE Sanskrit inscription by royal poet Ravikirti, which accurately dates the reign of Emperor Pulakeshin II and his legendary defeat of King Harshavardhana."
    ],
