    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/64/Kemmangundi_fog.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled",
    "description": "Cradled in the misty Western Ghats, Chikmagalur is the tranquil origin of India’s coffee legacy, crowned by Karnataka’s highest peak Mullayanagiri and rolling shola grasslands.",
    "longDescription": "In the 16th century, the Sufi saint Baba Budan smuggled seven raw coffee beans out of the port of Mocha in Yemen by wrapping them securely around his chest (as coffee was strictly guarded by Arab traders). He planted them on the slopes of the Chandradrona Hills, giving birth to the entire coffee heritage of India.\n\nChikmagalur (\"Town of the Younger Daughter\") was gifted as a wedding dowry by the legendary 9th-century chief Rukmangada to his younger daughter. The region is rich with ancient Hoysala temples in Amrithapura and Belavadi, hidden amidst deep rainforest gorges and perennial waterfalls like Hebbe and Baba Budangiri.\n\nChikmagalur invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Karnataka.",
    "loreTitle": "The Seven Magic Beans of Baba Budan & The Peak of Mullayanagiri",
    "ancientLegend": "In the 16th century, the Sufi saint Baba Budan smuggled seven raw coffee beans out of the port of Mocha in Yemen by wrapping them securely around his chest (as coffee was strictly guarded by Arab traders). He planted them on the slopes of the Chandradrona Hills, giving birth to the entire coffee heritage of India.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Chikmagalur (\"Town of the Younger Daughter\") was gifted as a wedding dowry by the legendary 9th-century chief Rukmangada to his younger daughter. The region is rich with ancient Hoysala temples in Amrithapura and Belavadi, hidden amidst deep rainforest gorges and perennial waterfalls like Hebbe and Baba Budangiri.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Kurinji Bloom of the Shola Hills: Every twelve years, the slopes of Mullayanagiri turn a breathtaking purple-blue when millions of wild Neelakurinji flowers burst into bloom all at once.",
      "The Cave of Baba Budan & Dattatreya: The high mountain shrine of Inam Dattatreya Peetha inside a natural rock cave has been worshipped jointly by both Hindu and Muslim devotees for five centuries in syncretic harmony."
    ],
