    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/dd/Delight_india.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled",
    "description": "Where rocky cliffs and palm-fringed beaches meet sacred Sanskrit agraharas, Gokarna is a timeless coastal pilgrimage town where pilgrims and backpackers share the sunset waves.",
    "longDescription": "When Ravana obtained the invincible Atmalinga from Lord Shiva on the condition that he never place it on the ground, the gods feared his invincibility. Lord Ganesha appeared as a young Brahmin boy and offered to hold the lingam while Ravana performed his evening prayers. Ganesha called Ravana’s name three times and set the lingam on the earth, where it rooted itself permanently. Ravana tried to yank it out with all his might, twisting it into the shape of a cow’s ear (Gokarna).\n\nThe Mahabaleshwar Temple dates back to the Kadamba dynasty (4th century CE) and is mentioned in the Tamil Sangam hymns. Beyond the town lie pristine hiking trails connecting Gokarna Main Beach, Kudle Beach, Om Beach (shaped like the sacred symbol ॐ), Half Moon Beach, and Paradise Beach.\n\nGokarna invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Karnataka.",
    "loreTitle": "The Trickery of Ganesha & The Mahabaleshwar Atmalinga",
    "ancientLegend": "When Ravana obtained the invincible Atmalinga from Lord Shiva on the condition that he never place it on the ground, the gods feared his invincibility. Lord Ganesha appeared as a young Brahmin boy and offered to hold the lingam while Ravana performed his evening prayers. Ganesha called Ravana’s name three times and set the lingam on the earth, where it rooted itself permanently. Ravana tried to yank it out with all his might, twisting it into the shape of a cow’s ear (Gokarna).",
    "historicalChronicle": "The Mahabaleshwar Temple dates back to the Kadamba dynasty (4th century CE) and is mentioned in the Tamil Sangam hymns. Beyond the town lie pristine hiking trails connecting Gokarna Main Beach, Kudle Beach, Om Beach (shaped like the sacred symbol ॐ), Half Moon Beach, and Paradise Beach.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Kotiteertha Holy Tank: The vast rectangular temple tank in the center of town is said to contain water from one crore (ten million) sacred rivers, where rituals for ancestors bring eternal peace to souls.",
      "The Singing Fishermen of Om Beach: Konkani fisherfolk tell of bioluminescent nights when schools of glowing fish leap out of the waters in rhythm with the sound of the evening temple conch shells."
    ],
