    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7d/Udupi_Sri_Krishna_Matha_Temple.jpg/1280px-Udupi_Sri_Krishna_Matha_Temple.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "A coastal temple city founded by the 13th-century philosopher saint Madhvacharya, famous for the Udupi Sri Krishna Matha, the Kanakana Kindi silver window, and authentic satvik Udupi cuisine.",
    "longDescription": "According to Dvaita lore, Saint Madhvacharya rescued a merchant ship stuck in a storm off the Malpe coast by waving his saffron shawl. In gratitude, the captain gave him a heavy lump of gopichandana clay used as ship ballast. When washed in the river, it revealed a magnificent ancient statue of child Krishna holding a churning rod, originally sculpted by Vishwakarma.\n\nIn the 16th century, the low-caste saint-poet Kanakadasa was forbidden from entering the temple to see the Lord. He stood behind the western outer wall playing his tambura and singing devotional songs. Miraculously, the temple wall cracked open and the Krishna idol rotated 180 degrees to face west, allowing Kanakadasa to see the Lord through a small window now known as Kanakana Kindi.\n\nUdupi invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Karnataka.",
    "loreTitle": "The Miracle of Kanakadasa & The Nine-Hole Silver Window",
    "ancientLegend": "According to Dvaita lore, Saint Madhvacharya rescued a merchant ship stuck in a storm off the Malpe coast by waving his saffron shawl. In gratitude, the captain gave him a heavy lump of gopichandana clay used as ship ballast. When washed in the river, it revealed a magnificent ancient statue of child Krishna holding a churning rod, originally sculpted by Vishwakarma.",
    "historicalChronicle": "In the 16th century, the low-caste saint-poet Kanakadasa was forbidden from entering the temple to see the Lord. He stood behind the western outer wall playing his tambura and singing devotional songs. Miraculously, the temple wall cracked open and the Krishna idol rotated 180 degrees to face west, allowing Kanakadasa to see the Lord through a small window now known as Kanakana Kindi.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Ever-Burning Kitchen of Ashta Mathas: Eight monastic lineages (Ashta Mathas) take turns every two years (Paryaya) to manage the temple and prepare free feasts for tens of thousands of pilgrims daily without modern electrical machinery.",
      "The Geometrical Black Basalt Pillars: At nearby St. Mary’s Islands, volcanic basalt rock formations stand in hexagonal vertical columns formed 88 million years ago when India broke away from Madagascar."
    ],
