    "description": "Spanning forty acres along the Malabar coastline, Bekal is Kerala’s largest and best-preserved coastal fortress, featuring an iconic keyhole-shaped layout and an observation tower looking out to sea.",
    "longDescription": "Folklore says the coastal headland was used by ancient Malabar chieftains to guard against Arab and Roman sea marauders. The name Bekal is derived from Baliakulam (\"Big Palace\"), representing a fortified coastal seat where ancient kings observed the seasonal monsoon swells.\n\nConstructed by Shivappa Nayaka of Keladi in 1650 CE, Bekal Fort was strategically designed not as an administrative palace, but purely for coastal defense. It contains no royal residential quarters—only ramparts, zigzag entrance gates designed to thwart war elephants, underground ammunition magazines, and a grand stepped water reservoir.\n\nBekal invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Kerala.",
    "loreTitle": "The Bastions of Shivappa Nayaka & The Water Tank of Bekal",
    "ancientLegend": "Folklore says the coastal headland was used by ancient Malabar chieftains to guard against Arab and Roman sea marauders. The name Bekal is derived from Baliakulam (\"Big Palace\"), representing a fortified coastal seat where ancient kings observed the seasonal monsoon swells.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Constructed by Shivappa Nayaka of Keladi in 1650 CE, Bekal Fort was strategically designed not as an administrative palace, but purely for coastal defense. It contains no royal residential quarters—only ramparts, zigzag entrance gates designed to thwart war elephants, underground ammunition magazines, and a grand stepped water reservoir.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Observation Tower Secret: The soaring circular tower in the center of the fort allows sentries to spot ships 30 kilometers away while remaining completely invisible behind the laterite gun-slits.",
      "The Hidden Sea Escape Tunnel: Local lore whispers of an escape tunnel that starts from the central water tank and exits directly into a sea cave beneath the crashing waves."
    ],
