    "description": "The historic capital of the powerful Zamorin rulers, Kozhikode (Calicut) is legendary for its hospitality, world-famous Malabar Biryani, Calico cotton weaving, and rich maritime trade.",
    "longDescription": "Arab, Chinese, and European merchant accounts praise the Zamorin rulers of Calicut for their absolute integrity: foreign traders could leave their gold chests unlocked in the market square with complete confidence that nothing would ever be stolen under the King’s law.\n\nOn May 20, 1498, Portuguese explorer Vasco da Gama landed at Kappad Beach near Kozhikode, permanently altering world trade routes. The city gave the English language the word \"Calico\" (from Calicut cotton) and remains the culinary heart of Kerala, famous for Kozhikodan Halwa and thin banana chips fried in coconut oil.\n\nKozhikode invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Kerala.",
    "loreTitle": "The Zamorin’s Truth & The Calico Weavers of Kozhikode",
    "ancientLegend": "Arab, Chinese, and European merchant accounts praise the Zamorin rulers of Calicut for their absolute integrity: foreign traders could leave their gold chests unlocked in the market square with complete confidence that nothing would ever be stolen under the King’s law.",
    "historicalChronicle": "On May 20, 1498, Portuguese explorer Vasco da Gama landed at Kappad Beach near Kozhikode, permanently altering world trade routes. The city gave the English language the word \"Calico\" (from Calicut cotton) and remains the culinary heart of Kerala, famous for Kozhikodan Halwa and thin banana chips fried in coconut oil.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Sweet Meat Street (SM Street): The bustling pedestrian bazaar of Mithai Theruvu was named after sweet-makers who produced translucent varieties of ghee-laden Halwa for centuries.",
      "The Mishkal Mosque: Built in the 14th century by wealthy Arab merchant Nakhooda Mishkal, this four-tiered timber mosque has no domes or minarets, built entirely in classical Kerala wooden temple architecture."
    ],
