    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Kumarkom.jpg/1280px-Kumarkom.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "A cluster of lush islands on Vembanad Lake, Kumarakom is a world-renowned backwater bird sanctuary where migratory Siberian storks, egrets, and kingfishers gather in mangrove canopies.",
    "longDescription": "Local legends recount that the vast backwaters of Vembanad were the sacred bathing waters of water nymphs (Yakshis) who protected the freshwater mangrove roots and ensured the lake never dried up even during blazing summer heat.\n\nIn 1847, English missionary Alfred George Baker reclaimed 500 acres of swamp land from Vembanad Lake, planting rubber, coconut trees, and mangrove forests that developed into the 14-acre Kumarakom Bird Sanctuary. The Baker heritage bungalow still stands beside the canals.\n\nKumarakom invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Kerala.",
    "loreTitle": "The Mangrove Paradise of Baker & The Waterfowl of Vembanad",
    "ancientLegend": "Local legends recount that the vast backwaters of Vembanad were the sacred bathing waters of water nymphs (Yakshis) who protected the freshwater mangrove roots and ensured the lake never dried up even during blazing summer heat.",
    "historicalChronicle": "In 1847, English missionary Alfred George Baker reclaimed 500 acres of swamp land from Vembanad Lake, planting rubber, coconut trees, and mangrove forests that developed into the 14-acre Kumarakom Bird Sanctuary. The Baker heritage bungalow still stands beside the canals.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Pearl Spot (Karimeen) Whisper: Fishermen catch the prized Karimeen fish using an ancient technique of diving underwater and locating the fish nested inside clay cavities by touch alone.",
      "The Siberian Crane Arrival: Villagers believe that the precise day migratory waterfowl arrive from Siberia in November heralds the start of prosperous winter harvests."
    ],
