    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b9/Munnar_Overview.jpg/1280px-Munnar_Overview.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "Nestled at 5,200 feet in the Western Ghats at the confluence of three mountain rivers (Muthirapuzha, Nallathanni, and Kundaly), Munnar is renowned for rolling emerald tea estates and the endangered Nilgiri Tahr.",
    "longDescription": "The indigenous Muthuvan tribal people (\"Those who carry on their back\") believe they migrated to these high peaks carrying the royal deities of the Madurai Pandyan dynasty on their backs during medieval wars. The surrounding Eravikulam National Park is the sanctuary of Anamudi (8,842 ft), the highest peak in South India.\n\nIn the late 19th century, British planter A.H. Sharp cleared the wild jungle to plant tea, transforming Munnar into one of the world’s highest tea-growing capitals. The CSI Christ Church, built of raw stone in 1910 with stained-glass memorial windows, preserves century-old brass plaques of pioneering planters.\n\nMunnar invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Kerala.",
    "loreTitle": "The Peak of Anamudi & The Purple Bloom of Neelakurinji",
    "ancientLegend": "The indigenous Muthuvan tribal people (\"Those who carry on their back\") believe they migrated to these high peaks carrying the royal deities of the Madurai Pandyan dynasty on their backs during medieval wars. The surrounding Eravikulam National Park is the sanctuary of Anamudi (8,842 ft), the highest peak in South India.",
    "historicalChronicle": "In the late 19th century, British planter A.H. Sharp cleared the wild jungle to plant tea, transforming Munnar into one of the world’s highest tea-growing capitals. The CSI Christ Church, built of raw stone in 1910 with stained-glass memorial windows, preserves century-old brass plaques of pioneering planters.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The 12-Year Neelakurinji Carpet: The hills of Munnar are the primary habitat of Strobilanthes kunthiana (Neelakurinji), which blankets the mountain slopes in vibrant lavender-blue once every 12 years.",
      "The Guardian of the Cliff: The endangered Nilgiri Tahr (mountain goat) scales vertical granite precipices at Eravikulam; tribal lore says they are blessed by mountain gods with suction-like hooves."
    ],
