    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/Leh_Palace_2011.jpg/1280px-Leh_Palace_2011.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "The historic capital of the Himalayan kingdom of Ladakh, nestled at 11,500 feet, celebrated for the 17th-century Leh Palace, Tibetan gompas, and bustling Old Town bazaars.",
    "longDescription": "Tibetan chronicles record that King Sengge Namgyal built the imposing 9-story Leh Palace in the 1600s, modeling it after the Potala Palace of Lhasa. The palace’s massive sloping mud, stone, and timber walls kept the royal family warm during minus-thirty-degree winters while overlooking the caravan routes of the Silk Road.\n\nLeh was the crucial trading crossroads where caravans laden with silk, tea, pashmina wool, turquoise, and spices converged from Yarkand, Kashgar, Tibet, and Kashmir. The Shanti Stupa, built on a hilltop in 1991 by Japanese monks, holds sacred relics of the Buddha looking toward the Stok Kangri peaks.\n\nLeh invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Ladakh.",
    "loreTitle": "The Kings of Maryul & The Nine-Storied Potala of Leh",
    "ancientLegend": "Tibetan chronicles record that King Sengge Namgyal built the imposing 9-story Leh Palace in the 1600s, modeling it after the Potala Palace of Lhasa. The palace’s massive sloping mud, stone, and timber walls kept the royal family warm during minus-thirty-degree winters while overlooking the caravan routes of the Silk Road.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Leh was the crucial trading crossroads where caravans laden with silk, tea, pashmina wool, turquoise, and spices converged from Yarkand, Kashgar, Tibet, and Kashmir. The Shanti Stupa, built on a hilltop in 1991 by Japanese monks, holds sacred relics of the Buddha looking toward the Stok Kangri peaks.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Donkey Sanctuary of Leh: Local volunteers maintain a sanctuary for retired pack donkeys, honoring the hardworking animals that sustained mountain trade for generations.",
      "The Tsemo Gompa Butter Lamp: High above Leh Palace atop Namgyal Tsemo peak sits an ancient fortress monastery containing an 8-meter gilded statue of Maitreya Buddha, where lamas light butter lamps that glow like stars against the midnight mountain silhouette."
    ],
