    "id": "loc_0080",
    "name": "Zanskar",
    "tagline": "The frozen river realm of cliff-hanging monasteries and chadar treks",
    "state": "Ladakh",
    "region": "Northern India",
    "category": "Adventure",
    "secondaryCategories": [
      "Culture",
      "Heritage",
      "Nature"
    ],
    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/75/Zanskar_Valley.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled",
    "description": "One of the most remote valleys in the Himalayas, Zanskar is a hidden kingdom of medieval cliffside monasteries, jagged gorges, and the legendary winter Chadar trek over frozen river ice.",
    "longDescription": "Zanskar (\"Copper Star\") remained cut off from the modern world for seven months each winter. When all mountain passes were buried under twenty feet of snow, the only lifeline was the Chadar—walking on the frozen sheet of ice atop the rushing Zanskar River through 1,000-foot vertical gorges.\n\nThe most extraordinary marvel is Phugtal Gompa, built in the 12th century directly inside the mouth of a massive natural limestone cave on a sheer cliffside above the Tsarap River. Founded by scholar Gangsem Sherap Sampo, it resembles a giant stone honeycomb housing eighty monks.\n\nZanskar invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Ladakh.",
    "loreTitle": "The Frozen River of Chadar & The Castle Monastery of Phugtal",
    "ancientLegend": "Zanskar (\"Copper Star\") remained cut off from the modern world for seven months each winter. When all mountain passes were buried under twenty feet of snow, the only lifeline was the Chadar—walking on the frozen sheet of ice atop the rushing Zanskar River through 1,000-foot vertical gorges.",
    "historicalChronicle": "The most extraordinary marvel is Phugtal Gompa, built in the 12th century directly inside the mouth of a massive natural limestone cave on a sheer cliffside above the Tsarap River. Founded by scholar Gangsem Sherap Sampo, it resembles a giant stone honeycomb housing eighty monks.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Cave of the Four Sages: Before the monastery was built, four enlightened Arhats are said to have meditated in the Phugtal cave, causing a miraculous spring to emerge from the rock wall that still flows today.",
      "The Ice Sheet Whisper: Chadar trekkers know that the ice \"sings\" as water flows underneath; experienced Zanskari guides can tell if the ice is thick enough to step on purely by listening to the pitch of the groan."
    ],
