    "id": "loc_0081",
    "name": "Bhimbetka",
    "tagline": "The 30,000-year-old rock shelters of prehistoric hunters and ochre artists",
    "state": "Madhya Pradesh",
    "region": "Central India",
    "category": "Heritage",
    "secondaryCategories": [
      "Culture",
      "Nature"
    ],
    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Rock_Shelter_8%2C_Bhimbetka_02.jpg/1280px-Rock_Shelter_8%2C_Bhimbetka_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "A UNESCO World Heritage sanctuary of over 750 rock shelters nestled in the Vindhyan sandstone hills, preserving 30,000 years of unbroken human artistic expression.",
    "longDescription": "The name Bhimbetka derives from \"Bhima-baithaka\" (\"The Sitting Place of Bhima\"), where the mighty Pandava warrior was said to have rested and watched over the ancient forests. When archaeologist V.S. Wakankar discovered the caves in 1957, he found prehistoric paintings layered across Paleolithic, Mesolithic, and historic eras.\n\nThe paintings depict dancing hunters, elephant riders, wild boars, and rhinoceroses drawn with natural mineral pigments of red ochre, white manganese, and vegetable gums that have survived tens of thousands of years inside natural sandstone overhangs.\n\nBhimbetka invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Madhya Pradesh.",
    "loreTitle": "The Seat of Bhima & The Paleolithic Ochre Galleries",
    "ancientLegend": "The name Bhimbetka derives from \"Bhima-baithaka\" (\"The Sitting Place of Bhima\"), where the mighty Pandava warrior was said to have rested and watched over the ancient forests. When archaeologist V.S. Wakankar discovered the caves in 1957, he found prehistoric paintings layered across Paleolithic, Mesolithic, and historic eras.",
    "historicalChronicle": "The paintings depict dancing hunters, elephant riders, wild boars, and rhinoceroses drawn with natural mineral pigments of red ochre, white manganese, and vegetable gums that have survived tens of thousands of years inside natural sandstone overhangs.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Zoo Rock Amphitheatre: One massive shelter, dubbed the \"Zoo Rock\", depicts 252 animals across 16 different species in vibrant detail, showcasing the prehistoric wildlife of ancient Central India.",
      "The Secret Pigment Recipe: Local Gond tribals still use identical crushed stone pigments mixed with animal fat and tree sap to paint ritual wall motifs (Digna) during harvest festivals."
    ],
