    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/24/Taj-ul-Masjid%2C_Bhopal%2C_India.jpg/1280px-Taj-ul-Masjid%2C_Bhopal%2C_India.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "The capital of Madhya Pradesh, Bhopal is a historic city of two vast lakes, narrow royal bazaars, and grand architectural landmarks built by four generations of female Muslim rulers (Begums).",
    "longDescription": "According to folklore, the Upper Lake (Bhojtal) was built in the 11th century by the scholar king Raja Bhoj of the Paramara dynasty to cure himself of a skin ailment using the waters of 365 mountain tributaries. Sages declared that bathing in the lake granted vitality and mental sharpness.\n\nFor over a century (1819–1926), Bhopal was ruled uninterruptedly by four successive female monarchs (Qudsia Begum, Sikandar Begum, Shah Jahan Begum, and Sultan Jahan Begum). They constructed the colossal Taj-ul-Masajid (\"Crown Among Mosques\") with its soaring pink minarets, modern waterworks, and public schools.\n\nBhopal invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Madhya Pradesh.",
    "loreTitle": "The Legacy of the Four Begums & The Grandeur of Taj-ul-Masajid",
    "ancientLegend": "According to folklore, the Upper Lake (Bhojtal) was built in the 11th century by the scholar king Raja Bhoj of the Paramara dynasty to cure himself of a skin ailment using the waters of 365 mountain tributaries. Sages declared that bathing in the lake granted vitality and mental sharpness.",
    "historicalChronicle": "For over a century (1819–1926), Bhopal was ruled uninterruptedly by four successive female monarchs (Qudsia Begum, Sikandar Begum, Shah Jahan Begum, and Sultan Jahan Begum). They constructed the colossal Taj-ul-Masajid (\"Crown Among Mosques\") with its soaring pink minarets, modern waterworks, and public schools.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Secret Sluice Gate of Kamla Park: Built by Gond queen Rani Kamlapati in the 18th century, the water palace featured secret underwater escape channels that allowed the queen to swim across the lake undetected during sieges.",
      "The Ittar Lane of Chowk: In the old walled city’s Chowk bazaar, traditional perfumers still distill rare natural fragrances like Mitti Ittar (the scent of baked earth after the first monsoon rain)."
    ],
