    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Dhuandhar_Waterfalls_in_Bhedaghat%2C_India.jpg/1280px-Dhuandhar_Waterfalls_in_Bhedaghat%2C_India.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "Situated on the banks of the sacred Narmada River, Jabalpur is world-famous for the soaring white marble canyons of Bhedaghat, the smoky Dhuandhar waterfall, and the 10th-century Chausath Yogini Temple.",
    "longDescription": "Sage Bhrigu meditated at Bhedaghat where the Narmada cuts through a two-kilometer canyon of pure white, pink, and dark grey marble. The Narmada is considered so sacred in Hindu lore that the mere sight of her water (Darshana) purifies a devotee of all lifetimes of karmic burden.\n\nThe Gond queen Rani Durgavati ruled the Garha-Katanga kingdom from the 12th-century Madan Mahal Fort atop a massive balancing rock. The 10th-century Chausath Yogini Temple features an open-air circular hypaethral cloister containing 64 intricately carved statues of yoginis riding various mystical vahanas.\n\nJabalpur invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Madhya Pradesh.",
    "loreTitle": "The Roar of Dhuandhar & The 64 Yoginis of the Circular Temple",
    "ancientLegend": "Sage Bhrigu meditated at Bhedaghat where the Narmada cuts through a two-kilometer canyon of pure white, pink, and dark grey marble. The Narmada is considered so sacred in Hindu lore that the mere sight of her water (Darshana) purifies a devotee of all lifetimes of karmic burden.",
    "historicalChronicle": "The Gond queen Rani Durgavati ruled the Garha-Katanga kingdom from the 12th-century Madan Mahal Fort atop a massive balancing rock. The 10th-century Chausath Yogini Temple features an open-air circular hypaethral cloister containing 64 intricately carved statues of yoginis riding various mystical vahanas.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Moonlight Boat Ride of Marble Rocks: On Sharad Purnima (autumn full moon), the white marble cliffs reflect the moonlight onto the mirror-calm river, creating an otherworldly glow that shifts with the boat’s ripples.",
      "The Balancing Rock of Madan Mahal: A colossal multi-ton granite boulder balances on a tiny contact point atop another rock, having survived a massive magnitude 6.0 earthquake in 1997 without shifting a millimeter."
    ],
