    "id": "loc_0096",
    "name": "Mahabaleshwar",
    "tagline": "The five sacred river sources atop the evergreen Sahyadri plateau",
    "state": "Maharashtra",
    "region": "Western India",
    "category": "Nature",
    "secondaryCategories": [
      "Culture",
      "Heritage"
    ],
    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/01/MAHABALESWAR_LANDSCAPE.jpg/1280px-MAHABALESWAR_LANDSCAPE.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "Perched at 4,400 feet in the Western Ghats, Mahabaleshwar is the strawberry capital of India, revered as the sacred origin of five holy rivers (Krishna, Venna, Koyna, Savitri, and Gayatri).",
    "longDescription": "According to the Skanda Purana, the twin demon brothers Mahabali and Atibali terrorized the earth. Lord Vishnu and Lord Shiva slew them after Goddess Maya mesmerized them. In his dying breath, Mahabali asked that the place be named after him (Mahabaleshwar) and that Lord Shiva reside here permanently as the Atibaleshwar Jyotirlinga.\n\nThe ancient Panchganga Temple houses a stone pool where water flows continuously from the carved mouth of a stone cow (Gomukh), said to be the mystical meeting point of the five sacred rivers. During the colonial era, Sir John Malcolm developed it as the summer capital of Bombay Presidency.\n\nMahabaleshwar invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Maharashtra.",
    "loreTitle": "The Five Springs of Panchganga & The Sunset of Arthur’s Seat",
    "ancientLegend": "According to the Skanda Purana, the twin demon brothers Mahabali and Atibali terrorized the earth. Lord Vishnu and Lord Shiva slew them after Goddess Maya mesmerized them. In his dying breath, Mahabali asked that the place be named after him (Mahabaleshwar) and that Lord Shiva reside here permanently as the Atibaleshwar Jyotirlinga.",
    "historicalChronicle": "The ancient Panchganga Temple houses a stone pool where water flows continuously from the carved mouth of a stone cow (Gomukh), said to be the mystical meeting point of the five sacred rivers. During the colonial era, Sir John Malcolm developed it as the summer capital of Bombay Presidency.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Floating Straw of Arthur’s Seat: At Arthur’s Seat point (4,400 ft), the intense thermal updrafts from the 2,000-foot Savitri valley below cause light objects like dry leaves or straw thrown off the cliff to float upwards into the sky.",
      "The Red Strawberry Revolution: British visitors first planted Australian strawberry runners here in the 1920s; today, the rich red volcanic laterite soil produces over 85% of India’s fresh strawberries."
    ],
