    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/12/Trimbakeshwar_Temple-Nashik-Maharashtra-1.jpg/1280px-Trimbakeshwar_Temple-Nashik-Maharashtra-1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "Resting on the banks of the sacred Godavari River (the \"Ganges of the South\"), Nashik is one of the four holy Kumbh Mela sites, home to the Trimbakeshwar Jyotirlinga, Panchavati forest lore, and lush vineyards.",
    "longDescription": "According to the Ramayana, it was in the Panchavati forest of Nashik that Lord Rama, Sita, and Lakshmana lived during exile. Lakshmana famously severed the nose (Nasika) of the demoness Surpanakha here, giving the city its name Nashik. At nearby Brahmagiri Hill, Sage Gautama’s intense penance persuaded Lord Shiva to release the sacred river Godavari onto earth.\n\nTrimbakeshwar Temple houses a unique three-faced lingam representing Brahma, Vishnu, and Shiva, adorned with a jewel-encrusted golden crown dating from the Pandavas. The city has transformed into the wine capital of India, with vineyard estates nestled in the foothills of the Sahyadris.\n\nNashik invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Maharashtra.",
    "loreTitle": "The Source of Kushavarta & The Trimbakeshwar Jyotirlinga",
    "ancientLegend": "According to the Ramayana, it was in the Panchavati forest of Nashik that Lord Rama, Sita, and Lakshmana lived during exile. Lakshmana famously severed the nose (Nasika) of the demoness Surpanakha here, giving the city its name Nashik. At nearby Brahmagiri Hill, Sage Gautama’s intense penance persuaded Lord Shiva to release the sacred river Godavari onto earth.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Trimbakeshwar Temple houses a unique three-faced lingam representing Brahma, Vishnu, and Shiva, adorned with a jewel-encrusted golden crown dating from the Pandavas. The city has transformed into the wine capital of India, with vineyard estates nestled in the foothills of the Sahyadris.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Kushavarta Holy Tank: Sage Gautama bound the rushing waters of the Godavari with holy Kusha grass into this rectangular stone pond, where millions of pilgrims take a holy dip during the Simhastha Kumbh Mela every 12 years.",
      "The Kalaram Temple Black Stone: The 1782 Kalaram Temple was built using two thousand tons of jet-black stone quarried from Ramshej, housing a rare black sand-carved murti of Lord Rama."
    ],
