    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e9/The_Loktak_Lake.jpg/1280px-The_Loktak_Lake.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "The largest freshwater lake in Northeast India, Loktak is world-famous for its circular floating islands of vegetation (Phumdis) and Keibul Lamjao—the only floating national park in the world.",
    "longDescription": "Manipuri Meitei mythology reveres Loktak Lake as the celestial mirror created by the supreme creator Soraren. The legendary epic of the star-crossed lovers Khamba and Thoibi is set along its shores, where tribal ballads sing of magic serpents (Pakhangba) guarding the deep crystal waters.\n\nThe floating islands, called Phumdis, are heterogeneous masses of soil, vegetation, and organic matter that float on the water table. The indigenous fisherfolk (Thanga community) build traditional floating huts called Phumsangs on these islands to live in harmony with the lake’s ecosystem.\n\nLoktak Lake invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Manipur.",
    "loreTitle": "The Floating Phumdis of Loktak & The Sanctuary of Sangai",
    "ancientLegend": "Manipuri Meitei mythology reveres Loktak Lake as the celestial mirror created by the supreme creator Soraren. The legendary epic of the star-crossed lovers Khamba and Thoibi is set along its shores, where tribal ballads sing of magic serpents (Pakhangba) guarding the deep crystal waters.",
    "historicalChronicle": "The floating islands, called Phumdis, are heterogeneous masses of soil, vegetation, and organic matter that float on the water table. The indigenous fisherfolk (Thanga community) build traditional floating huts called Phumsangs on these islands to live in harmony with the lake’s ecosystem.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Dancing Sangai Deer: Keibul Lamjao National Park is the last natural refuge of the endangered Eld’s deer (Sangai), whose delicate hooves balance gracefully on the sponge-like floating vegetation, earning it the title \"The Dancing Deer of Manipur\".",
      "The Sendra Island Panoramic View: From the summit of Sendra Island hill in the center of the lake, the floating Phumdis appear like emerald green rings scattered across a giant silver mirror."
    ],
