    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/6a/Umiam_Lake_-_by_Vikramjit_Kakati.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled",
    "description": "The capital of Meghalaya, Shillong is a cosmopolitan hill station celebrated for its pine-covered ridges, Umiam Lake (\"Barapani\"), colonial heritage cottages, and vibrant indie rock music culture.",
    "longDescription": "According to Khasi mythology, the city is named after U-Shyllong, a powerful deity born of a virgin mother who resided atop Shillong Peak (6,449 ft) to protect the surrounding hills from malevolent spirits. Sages believe the peak was the sacred umbilical cord connecting earth to heaven.\n\nEstablished as the administrative headquarters of British Assam in 1874, Shillong retains colonial landmarks like the All Saints Cathedral and the Ward’s Lake with its wooden horseshoe bridge. Umiam Lake, a colossal 220-square-kilometer reservoir, offers sailing, kayaking, and panoramic vistas across rolling pine ridges.\n\nShillong invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Meghalaya.",
    "loreTitle": "The Pine Ridges of Umiam & The Sacred Peak of Shillong",
    "ancientLegend": "According to Khasi mythology, the city is named after U-Shyllong, a powerful deity born of a virgin mother who resided atop Shillong Peak (6,449 ft) to protect the surrounding hills from malevolent spirits. Sages believe the peak was the sacred umbilical cord connecting earth to heaven.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Established as the administrative headquarters of British Assam in 1874, Shillong retains colonial landmarks like the All Saints Cathedral and the Ward’s Lake with its wooden horseshoe bridge. Umiam Lake, a colossal 220-square-kilometer reservoir, offers sailing, kayaking, and panoramic vistas across rolling pine ridges.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Tear of Two Sisters (Umiam): The name Umiam translates to \"Water of Tears\"; folklore tells of two celestial sisters who descended to earth—when one went missing, the grief-stricken sister wept so profusely that her tears formed the vast lake.",
      "The Archery Gambling of Teer: Every afternoon at the polo grounds, traditional Khasi archers shoot hundreds of arrows at a wooden target while locals bet on the last two digits of the total arrow count based on their dreams."
    ],
