    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Lingaraj_Temple_%2C_Bhubaneswar.jpg/1280px-Lingaraj_Temple_%2C_Bhubaneswar.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "The ancient capital of Kalinga, Bhubaneswar is home to over 700 medieval sandstone temples built between the 7th and 13th centuries, dominated by the majestic 180-foot spire of Lingaraja Temple.",
    "longDescription": "The Ekamra Kshetra (\"The Mango Forest\") was chosen by Lord Shiva as his favorite earthly retreat over Varanasi. The sacred lake of Bindusagar in the center of the city is said to contain water drops collected by Lord Shiva from every sacred river, lake, and ocean in the universe to quench Goddess Parvati’s thirst.\n\nBhubaneswar preserves the complete architectural evolution of Kalinga temple art: from the 7th-century Parasurameswara Temple with its low tiered roof, to the gem-like 10th-century Mukteswara Temple (\"The Gem of Odishan Architecture\") with its exquisite Torana arch, to the monumental Lingaraja Temple.\n\nBhubaneswar invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Odisha.",
    "loreTitle": "The Bindusagar Holy Waters & The Lingaraja Shikhara",
    "ancientLegend": "The Ekamra Kshetra (\"The Mango Forest\") was chosen by Lord Shiva as his favorite earthly retreat over Varanasi. The sacred lake of Bindusagar in the center of the city is said to contain water drops collected by Lord Shiva from every sacred river, lake, and ocean in the universe to quench Goddess Parvati’s thirst.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Bhubaneswar preserves the complete architectural evolution of Kalinga temple art: from the 7th-century Parasurameswara Temple with its low tiered roof, to the gem-like 10th-century Mukteswara Temple (\"The Gem of Odishan Architecture\") with its exquisite Torana arch, to the monumental Lingaraja Temple.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Harihara Synergy of Lingaraja: The presiding deity of Lingaraja is worshipped uniquely as Harihara—half Vishnu and half Shiva—symbolized by offerings of both bilva leaves (for Shiva) and tulsi leaves (for Vishnu).",
      "The Jain Monk Caves of Udayagiri: On the city’s outskirts rise the twin hills of Udayagiri and Khandagiri, where 2nd-century BCE Jain ascetics carved 33 rock-cut dwelling cells, including the famous Queen’s Palace cave (Rani Gumpha)."
    ],
