    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/94/Birds_eyeview_of_Chilika_Lake.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled",
    "description": "Asia’s largest brackish water lagoon, Chilika Lake is an ecological paradise of migratory flamingos, emerald islands, and the rare, playful snub-finned Irrawaddy dolphins.",
    "longDescription": "Local folklore speaks of a young girl named Jaai who was traveling across the lake by boat to her wedding on a stormy evening. A sudden squall capsized the boat near a rocky island; Jaai drowned and became Goddess Kalijai, the revered guardian spirit of Chilika who protects fishermen and sailors from deadly squalls.\n\nCovering up to 1,100 square kilometers, Chilika was a bustling port two thousand years ago from which ancient Kalinga mariners (Sadhabas) sailed colossal wooden merchant ships (Boitas) across the Bay of Bengal to trade in Java, Sumatra, and Bali during the annual Boita Bandana festival.\n\nChilika invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Odisha.",
    "loreTitle": "The Island of Kalijai & The Playful Irrawaddy Dolphins",
    "ancientLegend": "Local folklore speaks of a young girl named Jaai who was traveling across the lake by boat to her wedding on a stormy evening. A sudden squall capsized the boat near a rocky island; Jaai drowned and became Goddess Kalijai, the revered guardian spirit of Chilika who protects fishermen and sailors from deadly squalls.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Covering up to 1,100 square kilometers, Chilika was a bustling port two thousand years ago from which ancient Kalinga mariners (Sadhabas) sailed colossal wooden merchant ships (Boitas) across the Bay of Bengal to trade in Java, Sumatra, and Bali during the annual Boita Bandana festival.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Whispering Irrawaddy Dolphins of Satapada: At Satapada where the lagoon meets the open sea, pods of rare Irrawaddy dolphins breach the surface to herd fish into fishermen’s nets in a symbiotic tradition.",
      "The Nalabana Bird Island: During winter, the 15-square-kilometer island of Nalabana submerges under water, leaving only lush mangrove tips where over a million migratory birds from the Caspian Sea and Lake Baikal roost."
    ],
