    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/94/The_Golden_Temple_of_Amrithsar_7.jpg/1280px-The_Golden_Temple_of_Amrithsar_7.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "The spiritual and cultural capital of Sikhism, Amritsar is home to the radiant Sri Harmandir Sahib (The Golden Temple), the sacred pool of nectar, and the largest free community kitchen on Earth.",
    "longDescription": "The fourth Sikh Guru, Guru Ram Das, founded the city in 1577 by digging the sacred pool of nectar (Amrit Sarovar), which was believed since ancient times to possess divine healing powers. Guru Arjan Dev designed Harmandir Sahib with four entrance doors open to the four cardinal directions, signifying that people of all castes, creeds, and faiths are equally welcome.\n\nIn 1830, Maharaja Ranjit Singh gilded the upper sanctum with 500 kilograms of pure gold foil, transforming it into the Golden Temple. Nearby lies Jallianwala Bagh, where the tragic 1919 massacre spurred India’s final struggle for independence; bullet marks still remain visible on the brick walls.\n\nAmritsar invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Punjab.",
    "loreTitle": "The Pool of Amrit & The Golden Domes of Harmandir Sahib",
    "ancientLegend": "The fourth Sikh Guru, Guru Ram Das, founded the city in 1577 by digging the sacred pool of nectar (Amrit Sarovar), which was believed since ancient times to possess divine healing powers. Guru Arjan Dev designed Harmandir Sahib with four entrance doors open to the four cardinal directions, signifying that people of all castes, creeds, and faiths are equally welcome.",
    "historicalChronicle": "In 1830, Maharaja Ranjit Singh gilded the upper sanctum with 500 kilograms of pure gold foil, transforming it into the Golden Temple. Nearby lies Jallianwala Bagh, where the tragic 1919 massacre spurred India’s final struggle for independence; bullet marks still remain visible on the brick walls.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The 100,000 Free Meals of the Mega Langar: The Golden Temple’s community kitchen (Guru Ram Das Langar) serves over 100,000 free hot vegetarian meals every single day of the year, prepared entirely by selfless volunteers (Sevadars).",
      "The Dukh Bhanjani Beri Tree: On the eastern edge of the sacred pool stands a 450-year-old jujube tree (Dukh Bhanjani Beri); according to legend, a leper who bathed in the pool beside this tree was cured of his affliction."
    ],
