    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/99/Mehrangarh_Fort_sanhita.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled",
    "description": "Dominated by the colossal 400-foot clifftop Mehrangarh Fort, Jodhpur (\"The Sun City\") is a sea of indigo-blue painted houses, royal cenotaphs at Jaswant Thada, and bustling spice bazaars.",
    "longDescription": "When Rao Jodha began construction of Mehrangarh Fort in 1459 atop the rocky cliff of Bhakurcheeria (\"Mountain of Birds\"), he displaced an ascetic hermit named Cheeria Nathji. The enraged sage cursed the kingdom: \"May your citadel suffer perennial droughts!\" To ward off the curse, a devoted citizen named Raja Ram Meghwal voluntarily agreed to be buried alive in the foundation stones to bless the fortress with eternal strength.\n\nRudyard Kipling described Mehrangarh as \"a palace that might have been built by Titans and colored by the morning sun\". Its battlements still bear the cannonball indentations from historic sieges. The indigo-blue wash on Brahmin houses in the old quarter was originally used to deter termites and reflect the harsh desert heat.\n\nJodhpur invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Rajasthan.",
    "loreTitle": "The Blue Alleys of the Brahmins & The Citadel of the Sun",
    "ancientLegend": "When Rao Jodha began construction of Mehrangarh Fort in 1459 atop the rocky cliff of Bhakurcheeria (\"Mountain of Birds\"), he displaced an ascetic hermit named Cheeria Nathji. The enraged sage cursed the kingdom: \"May your citadel suffer perennial droughts!\" To ward off the curse, a devoted citizen named Raja Ram Meghwal voluntarily agreed to be buried alive in the foundation stones to bless the fortress with eternal strength.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Rudyard Kipling described Mehrangarh as \"a palace that might have been built by Titans and colored by the morning sun\". Its battlements still bear the cannonball indentations from historic sieges. The indigo-blue wash on Brahmin houses in the old quarter was originally used to deter termites and reflect the harsh desert heat.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Handprints of Queen Sati: At the iron gate (Loha Pol) of Mehrangarh, 31 silver-plated palm prints of royal widows mark their departure for the funeral pyre in 1843.",
      "The Flying Foxes of Chamunda Temple: The Chamunda Devi temple atop the fort’s northern cliff is believed to have saved the city from aerial bombings during the 1965 and 1971 conflicts through the divine protection of the goddess."
    ],
