    "id": "loc_0123",
    "name": "Mandawa",
    "tagline": "The open-air art gallery of frescoed merchant mansions in Shekhawati",
    "state": "Rajasthan",
    "region": "Western India",
    "category": "Culture",
    "secondaryCategories": [
      "Heritage",
      "Nature"
    ],
    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/0b/Haveli_mandawa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled",
    "description": "Known as the \"Open-Air Art Gallery of Rajasthan\", Mandawa is an 18th-century trading town in the Shekhawati region, renowned for hundreds of grand Havelis covered in vibrant wall frescoes.",
    "longDescription": "Founded in the mid-18th century as a desert trading outpost along caravan routes between Delhi and the ports of Gujarat, Mandawa was fortified by Nawal Singh. The wealthy Marwari merchant clans (Banias) competed with each other to commission the most extravagant wall murals on their mansions to showcase their wealth.\n\nThe frescoes at Murmuria Haveli, Hanuman Prasad Goenka Haveli, and Mandawa Castle depict an astonishing fusion of mythological epics, Rajasthani folk ballads, and whimsical European inventions like steam locomotives, gramophones, airplanes, and British soldiers with pocket watches.\n\nMandawa invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Rajasthan.",
    "loreTitle": "The Opulent Havelis of the Silk Merchants & The Wall Frescoes of Mandawa",
    "ancientLegend": "Founded in the mid-18th century as a desert trading outpost along caravan routes between Delhi and the ports of Gujarat, Mandawa was fortified by Nawal Singh. The wealthy Marwari merchant clans (Banias) competed with each other to commission the most extravagant wall murals on their mansions to showcase their wealth.",
    "historicalChronicle": "The frescoes at Murmuria Haveli, Hanuman Prasad Goenka Haveli, and Mandawa Castle depict an astonishing fusion of mythological epics, Rajasthani folk ballads, and whimsical European inventions like steam locomotives, gramophones, airplanes, and British soldiers with pocket watches.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Natural Mineral Pigments: Shekhawati fresco artists (Chiteras) used crushed gemstone powder, lapis lazuli, lime, and kohl to paint on wet plaster (A fresco-buono technique), ensuring the colors remained brilliant for centuries without fading.",
      "The Double-Lock Vaults of the Havelis: Wealthy merchant havelis featured hidden underground vaults (Tijoris) protected by brass combination locks that required four keys held by different family elders to open."
    ],
