    "id": "loc_0125",
    "name": "Ranthambore",
    "tagline": "The ancient jungle fortress where Royal Bengal tigers prowl amidst ruined cenotaphs",
    "state": "Rajasthan",
    "region": "Western India",
    "category": "Wildlife",
    "secondaryCategories": [
      "Culture",
      "Heritage",
      "Nature"
    ],
    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/52/Ranthambhore_Fort.jpg/1280px-Ranthambhore_Fort.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "A dramatic wilderness where the Aravalli and Vindhya mountain ranges meet, Ranthambore is India’s most famous tiger reserve, dominated by the 10th-century UNESCO World Heritage Ranthambore Fort.",
    "longDescription": "Founded in 944 CE by the Nagil Jats and expanded by Chauhan kings, the fort was ruled by the legendary hero Rana Hammir Dev, who sacrificed his life rather than surrender his refugee guests to Alauddin Khilji in 1301. Sages worship Lord Ganesha at the Trinetra Ganesha Temple—the only temple where Ganesha has three eyes.\n\nRanthambore is unique because Royal Bengal tigers hunt and raise cubs amidst 1,000-year-old stone chhatris, water palaces, and hunting pavilions around Padam Talao and Rajbagh lakes. It was home to \"Machli\" (T-16), the legendary Queen of Tigers who lived to the record age of twenty and famously defeated a 14-foot crocodile.\n\nRanthambore invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Rajasthan.",
    "loreTitle": "The Fortress of Hammir Dev & The Lake of Machli the Queen Tiger",
    "ancientLegend": "Founded in 944 CE by the Nagil Jats and expanded by Chauhan kings, the fort was ruled by the legendary hero Rana Hammir Dev, who sacrificed his life rather than surrender his refugee guests to Alauddin Khilji in 1301. Sages worship Lord Ganesha at the Trinetra Ganesha Temple—the only temple where Ganesha has three eyes.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Ranthambore is unique because Royal Bengal tigers hunt and raise cubs amidst 1,000-year-old stone chhatris, water palaces, and hunting pavilions around Padam Talao and Rajbagh lakes. It was home to \"Machli\" (T-16), the legendary Queen of Tigers who lived to the record age of twenty and famously defeated a 14-foot crocodile.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Postal Letters to Trinetra Ganesha: Devotees from all over India mail thousands of wedding invitations and letters directly to \"Trinetra Ganesha, Ranthambore Fort\"; the postal department delivers bags of real mail to the temple priests every single day.",
      "The Padam Talao Jauhar: At Padam Talao lake, Queen Ranga Devi led the women into the water in a tragic Jauhar when the fort fell in 1301."
    ],
