    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Rumtek_Monastery_alias_Dharma_Chakra_Centre_near_Gangtok%2C_East_Sikkim_09.jpg/1280px-Rumtek_Monastery_alias_Dharma_Chakra_Centre_near_Gangtok%2C_East_Sikkim_09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "Perched at 5,410 feet on a misty Himalayan ridge, Gangtok is the serene capital of Sikkim, renowned for Tibetan Buddhist culture, orchid sanctuaries, and panoramic views of Mount Kanchenjunga.",
    "longDescription": "Sikkim’s indigenous Lepcha and Bhutia communities revere Mount Kanchenjunga (Kangchendzonga, \"Five Treasures of the High Snow\") as the sacred home of their guardian deity who protects the valleys with five celestial stores of salt, gold, turquoise, sacred scriptures, and medicine.\n\nIn the mid-19th century, Chogyal Thutob Namgyal moved the royal capital to Gangtok. Enchey Monastery was built on a hilltop blessed by the tantric flying master Lama Druptob Karpo. The Namgyal Institute of Tibetology preserves one of the world’s most extensive collections of Tibetan manuscripts and rare bronze statues.\n\nGangtok invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Sikkim.",
    "loreTitle": "The Enchey Monastery of Tantric Masters & The View of the Five Treasures",
    "ancientLegend": "Sikkim’s indigenous Lepcha and Bhutia communities revere Mount Kanchenjunga (Kangchendzonga, \"Five Treasures of the High Snow\") as the sacred home of their guardian deity who protects the valleys with five celestial stores of salt, gold, turquoise, sacred scriptures, and medicine.",
    "historicalChronicle": "In the mid-19th century, Chogyal Thutob Namgyal moved the royal capital to Gangtok. Enchey Monastery was built on a hilltop blessed by the tantric flying master Lama Druptob Karpo. The Namgyal Institute of Tibetology preserves one of the world’s most extensive collections of Tibetan manuscripts and rare bronze statues.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Cham Mask Dance of Enchey: During the annual festival of Kagyed, monks wearing elaborate painted masks of Mahakala and protective wrathful deities perform sacred trance dances to banish evil energies.",
      "The Orchid Wonderland: Sikkim is home to over 500 species of wild orchids; local lore says the rare blue Vanda orchid blossoms only where the mountain air is of supreme spiritual purity."
    ],
