    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/55/Panoramic_view_of_large_statue_of_Guru_Padmasambhava_%28Guru_Rinpoche%29m_Namchi.jpg/1280px-Panoramic_view_of_large_statue_of_Guru_Padmasambhava_%28Guru_Rinpoche%29m_Namchi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "Perched on the southern ridges of Sikkim overlooking the Rangit valley, Namchi (\"Sky High\") is a major pilgrimage center dominated by the colossal 135-foot statue of Guru Padmasambhava at Samdruptse.",
    "longDescription": "Samdruptse Hill (\"The Wish-Fulfilling Hill\") was believed by local lamas to be an active volcano that was dormant only because holy ascetics prayed atop it. To appease the subterranean mountain spirits, the 135-foot monumental gilded copper statue of Guru Padmasambhava was constructed atop the ridge in 2004.\n\nAt Solophok Hill, the Siddhesvara Dham complex was established with an 87-foot colossal statue of Lord Shiva encircled by replicas of the four supreme Char Dham temples (Badrinath, Jagannath, Dwarka, and Rameswaram) and the twelve Jyotirlingas.\n\nNamchi invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Sikkim.",
    "loreTitle": "The 135-Foot Golden Guru & The Solophok Char Dham",
    "ancientLegend": "Samdruptse Hill (\"The Wish-Fulfilling Hill\") was believed by local lamas to be an active volcano that was dormant only because holy ascetics prayed atop it. To appease the subterranean mountain spirits, the 135-foot monumental gilded copper statue of Guru Padmasambhava was constructed atop the ridge in 2004.",
    "historicalChronicle": "At Solophok Hill, the Siddhesvara Dham complex was established with an 87-foot colossal statue of Lord Shiva encircled by replicas of the four supreme Char Dham temples (Badrinath, Jagannath, Dwarka, and Rameswaram) and the twelve Jyotirlingas.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Wish-Fulfilling Hill: Pilgrims believe that making three clockwise circumambulations around the base of the Samdruptse statue while chanting \"Om Ah Hum Vajra Guru Padma Siddhi Hum\" dispels all karmic obstacles.",
      "The Ngadak Monastery Stones: Built during the reign of Chogyal Gyurmed Namgyal, Ngadak Monastery was constructed from mud and stone that was carried by royal courtiers from four sacred directions."
    ],
