    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Main_Shrine_of_Pemangytse_Gompa_with_prayer_flags.jpg/1280px-Main_Shrine_of_Pemangytse_Gompa_with_prayer_flags.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "A scenic hill town in West Sikkim celebrated for the historic Pemayangtse Monastery, the royal palace ruins of Rabdentse, and breathtaking front-row vistas of Mount Kanchenjunga.",
    "longDescription": "In 1642 CE, three venerable lamas from different parts of Tibet converged at Yuksom according to prophecy and crowned Phuntsog Namgyal as the first Chogyal (Dharma King) of Sikkim, consecrating him on the stone throne of Norbugang.\n\nPemayangtse Monastery (\"Sublime Perfect Lotus\"), founded in 1705, is one of the oldest and premier monasteries of the Nyingma order. Its top floor houses the Sangtokpalri—a breathtaking 7-tiered wooden sculpture carved over five years by a single master lama depicting the celestial heavenly paradise of Guru Rinpoche.\n\nPelling invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Sikkim.",
    "loreTitle": "The Consecration of Yuksom & The Ruins of Rabdentse",
    "ancientLegend": "In 1642 CE, three venerable lamas from different parts of Tibet converged at Yuksom according to prophecy and crowned Phuntsog Namgyal as the first Chogyal (Dharma King) of Sikkim, consecrating him on the stone throne of Norbugang.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Pemayangtse Monastery (\"Sublime Perfect Lotus\"), founded in 1705, is one of the oldest and premier monasteries of the Nyingma order. Its top floor houses the Sangtokpalri—a breathtaking 7-tiered wooden sculpture carved over five years by a single master lama depicting the celestial heavenly paradise of Guru Rinpoche.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Rabdentse Royal Palace Ruins: The palace of Rabdentse was destroyed by invading Nepalese Gorkha armies in the 18th century; today, its stone foundations stand like a forgotten Mayan citadel overlooking the mist-filled valley.",
      "The Wish-Fulfilling Lake of Khecheopalri: A short drive away lies Khecheopalri Lake, considered sacred by both Buddhists and Hindus; according to folklore, birds never allow even a single leaf to float on the lake surface, picking up every fallen leaf immediately."
    ],
