    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/99/Kapaleeswarar1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled",
    "description": "The gateway to South India, Chennai is a vibrant coastal metropolis celebrated for classical Bharatanatyam dance, Carnatic music December Season, Marina Beach, and ancient Chola-Pallava temples.",
    "longDescription": "According to myth, Goddess Parvati was cursed to be born on earth as a peahen (Mayil) for distracted meditation. She worshipped Lord Shiva under a Punnai tree on the seashore at Mylapore until the Lord redeemed her and took the name Kapaleeshwarar. Mylapore (\"City of Peacocks\") was celebrated in the hymns of 7th-century Saivite Nayanars.\n\nChennai evolved from the fishing village of Madraspatnam, where the British East India Company built Fort St. George in 1644—their first fortified coastal outpost in India. Marina Beach spans 13 kilometers as the world’s second longest urban beach, lined with statues of Tamil literary legends.\n\nChennai invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Tamil Nadu.",
    "loreTitle": "The 2,000-Year-Old Kapaleeshwarar Temple & The Marina Promenade",
    "ancientLegend": "According to myth, Goddess Parvati was cursed to be born on earth as a peahen (Mayil) for distracted meditation. She worshipped Lord Shiva under a Punnai tree on the seashore at Mylapore until the Lord redeemed her and took the name Kapaleeshwarar. Mylapore (\"City of Peacocks\") was celebrated in the hymns of 7th-century Saivite Nayanars.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Chennai evolved from the fishing village of Madraspatnam, where the British East India Company built Fort St. George in 1644—their first fortified coastal outpost in India. Marina Beach spans 13 kilometers as the world’s second longest urban beach, lined with statues of Tamil literary legends.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Tomb of St. Thomas the Apostle: San Thome Cathedral Basilica is one of only three basilicas in the world built directly over the tomb of an apostle of Jesus Christ (the other two being St. Peter’s in Rome and Santiago de Compostela in Spain).",
      "The Filter Coffee Secret of Mylapore: Traditional Brahmin coffee houses brew decoction from dark-roasted chicory beans in brass drip filters, served in bronze Davarah-tumbler sets frothed from arm’s length into a velvety foam."
    ],
