    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/The_Chettinad_palace_karaikudi_2013-09-07_00-11.jpg/1280px-The_Chettinad_palace_karaikudi_2013-09-07_00-11.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "A fascinating heritage region of 74 villages, Chettinad is famous for palatial 19th-century merchant mansions, handmade geometric Athangudi floor tiles, and spicy culinary traditions.",
    "longDescription": "The Chettiar (Nagarathar) merchant community originally lived as seafaring salt and gem traders in the Chola coastal port of Kaveripoompattinam (Poompuhar). When a catastrophic tsunami flooded the ancient port in the 8th century, the Chola king granted them land in the dry interior of Chettinad, where they reinvented themselves as international financiers.\n\nIn the 19th and early 20th centuries, Chettiar bankers financed trade across Burma, Ceylon, Malaya, and Vietnam. They channeled their vast fortunes into building sprawling mansions (Valavu Veedu) with Italian marble pillars, Belgian mirrors, Burmese teak beams, and hundreds of carved doors.\n\nChettinad invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Tamil Nadu.",
    "loreTitle": "The Thousand-Window Mansions of Nagarathars & The Athangudi Tiles",
    "ancientLegend": "The Chettiar (Nagarathar) merchant community originally lived as seafaring salt and gem traders in the Chola coastal port of Kaveripoompattinam (Poompuhar). When a catastrophic tsunami flooded the ancient port in the 8th century, the Chola king granted them land in the dry interior of Chettinad, where they reinvented themselves as international financiers.",
    "historicalChronicle": "In the 19th and early 20th centuries, Chettiar bankers financed trade across Burma, Ceylon, Malaya, and Vietnam. They channeled their vast fortunes into building sprawling mansions (Valavu Veedu) with Italian marble pillars, Belgian mirrors, Burmese teak beams, and hundreds of carved doors.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Hand-Pressed Athangudi Tiles: Master craftsmen in Athangudi village still cast colorful floor tiles individually by pouring liquid mineral pigments into glass molds and pressing them with river sand and cement.",
      "The 18-Spice Chettinad Masala: Authentic Chettinad cuisine uses rare roasted spices like star anise, stone flower (Kalpasi), and Marathi Moggu ground manually on stone pestles (Ammikkallu)."
    ],
