    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/19/7th_century_Sri_Kailashnathar_Temple_Kanchipuram_Tamil_Nadu_India_01_%2811%29.jpg/1280px-7th_century_Sri_Kailashnathar_Temple_Kanchipuram_Tamil_Nadu_India_01_%2811%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "One of the seven sacred Moksha-giving cities of India and ancient capital of the Pallava dynasty, Kanchipuram is world-famous for grand Dravidian temples and hand-woven pure Kanchipuram silk sarees.",
    "longDescription": "At the ancient Ekambareswarar Temple, Goddess Parvati created a Shivling out of loose sand (Prithvi Lingam) and worshipped it. When the Vegavathi river flooded, she embraced the lingam to protect it from being washed away; the idol still bears the gentle impression of the Goddess’s bangles and embrace.\n\nKanchipuram was a bustling metropolis of Sanskrit and Tamil learning where Buddhist scholar Bodhidharma (who introduced Zen Buddhism and Shaolin martial arts to China) was born. The Kailasanathar Temple, built in 700 CE by Pallava King Rajasimha, is the oldest stone structural temple in South India.\n\nKanchipuram invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Tamil Nadu.",
    "loreTitle": "The Earth Lingam of Ekambareswarar & The Golden Threads of Kanchipuram",
    "ancientLegend": "At the ancient Ekambareswarar Temple, Goddess Parvati created a Shivling out of loose sand (Prithvi Lingam) and worshipped it. When the Vegavathi river flooded, she embraced the lingam to protect it from being washed away; the idol still bears the gentle impression of the Goddess’s bangles and embrace.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Kanchipuram was a bustling metropolis of Sanskrit and Tamil learning where Buddhist scholar Bodhidharma (who introduced Zen Buddhism and Shaolin martial arts to China) was born. The Kailasanathar Temple, built in 700 CE by Pallava King Rajasimha, is the oldest stone structural temple in South India.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The 3,500-Year-Old Mango Tree: In the courtyard of Ekambareswarar stands a revered ancient mango tree whose four branches are said to yield mangoes of four distinct flavors corresponding to the four Vedas.",
      "The Pure Zari Silk Tradition: Kanchipuram silk sarees are woven on double-shuttle wooden looms using three individual threads of mulberry silk intertwined with pure silver wire dipped in 24-karat gold."
    ],
