    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Boating_in_Kodaikanal_Lake_with_Mist.jpg/1280px-Boating_in_Kodaikanal_Lake_with_Mist.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "Perched at 7,000 feet in the granite Palani Hills of the Western Ghats, Kodaikanal (\"The Gift of the Forest\") is famous for its star-shaped man-made lake, eucalyptus forests, and Pillar Rocks.",
    "longDescription": "The indigenous Palaiyar tribal people inhabited these high misty ridges for millennia, worshipping the mountain deity Lord Murugan at the Kurinji Andavar Temple, named after the sacred Neelakurinji flower that carpets the mountain slopes in purple-blue blossoms every twelve years.\n\nKodaikanal was established in 1845 by American Christian missionaries and British officials seeking refuge from tropical malaria in the plains. In 1863, Sir Vere Henry Levinge created the iconic 60-acre star-shaped Kodaikanal Lake by damming three mountain streams.\n\nKodaikanal invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Tamil Nadu.",
    "loreTitle": "The Star Lake of Kodaikanal & The Kurinji Flowers of Coaker’s Walk",
    "ancientLegend": "The indigenous Palaiyar tribal people inhabited these high misty ridges for millennia, worshipping the mountain deity Lord Murugan at the Kurinji Andavar Temple, named after the sacred Neelakurinji flower that carpets the mountain slopes in purple-blue blossoms every twelve years.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Kodaikanal was established in 1845 by American Christian missionaries and British officials seeking refuge from tropical malaria in the plains. In 1863, Sir Vere Henry Levinge created the iconic 60-acre star-shaped Kodaikanal Lake by damming three mountain streams.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Echo of the Pillar Rocks: Three vertical granite boulder cliffs stand 400 feet high like sentinels; local lore says mountain hawks nest in the deep chasms between the pillars where mist billows like smoke.",
      "The Guna Caves (Devil’s Kitchen): Deep bat-inhabited rock chasms between massive boulders were named the Devil’s Kitchen, surrounded by ancient exposed tree roots that look like sleeping serpents."
    ],
