    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/62/The_tank_and_Rockfort_Trichinopoly.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled",
    "description": "Situated on the Cauvery River, Tiruchirappalli (Trichy) is dominated by the dramatic 273-foot Rock Fort Temple and Sri Ranganathaswamy Temple at Srirangam—the largest functioning Hindu temple complex on Earth.",
    "longDescription": "According to myth, Lord Ganesha disguised himself as a small Brahmin boy to prevent the demon king Vibhishana from carrying the deity of Lord Ranganatha to Lanka. When Vibhishana realized the trick, he chased the boy up the massive rock and tapped him on the head; the small dent is still visible on the Ucchi Pillayar Ganesha idol atop the summit.\n\nThe Rock Fort is carved out of an ancient quartzite rock formation estimated by geologists to be 3.8 billion years old—older than the Himalayas. Across the river lies Srirangam, spanning 156 acres enclosed by seven concentric rectangular stone walls (Prakaras) with 21 soaring gopurams, including the 236-foot Rajagopuram.\n\nTiruchirappalli invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Tamil Nadu.",
    "loreTitle": "The Rock Fort of Ucchi Pillayar & The Island Temple of Srirangam",
    "ancientLegend": "According to myth, Lord Ganesha disguised himself as a small Brahmin boy to prevent the demon king Vibhishana from carrying the deity of Lord Ranganatha to Lanka. When Vibhishana realized the trick, he chased the boy up the massive rock and tapped him on the head; the small dent is still visible on the Ucchi Pillayar Ganesha idol atop the summit.",
    "historicalChronicle": "The Rock Fort is carved out of an ancient quartzite rock formation estimated by geologists to be 3.8 billion years old—older than the Himalayas. Across the river lies Srirangam, spanning 156 acres enclosed by seven concentric rectangular stone walls (Prakaras) with 21 soaring gopurams, including the 236-foot Rajagopuram.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The 437 Steps to the Sky: Climbing the 437 stone steps chiseled through the interior rock tunnel leads to the summit temple, offering panoramic 360-degree views of the green Cauvery delta.",
      "The Thousand-Pillared Granite Mandapam of Srirangam: The grand pavilion features life-sized charging war horses carved from single blocks of granite with warrior riders wielding spears and shields."
    ],
