    "id": "loc_0149",
    "name": "Fatehpur Sikri",
    "tagline": "The red sandstone city of victory and the soaring gate of Emperor Akbar",
    "state": "Uttar Pradesh",
    "region": "Northern India",
    "category": "Heritage",
    "secondaryCategories": [
      "Culture",
      "Nature"
    ],
    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Fatehput_Sikiri_Buland_Darwaza_gate_2010.jpg/1280px-Fatehput_Sikiri_Buland_Darwaza_gate_2010.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "A UNESCO World Heritage ghost city of red sandstone palaces built by Emperor Akbar in 1571, home to the white marble Dargah of Salim Chishti and the 54-meter Buland Darwaza.",
    "longDescription": "Desperate for an heir, Emperor Akbar walked barefoot from Agra to the rocky ridge of Sikri to seek the blessings of the Sufi saint Sheikh Salim Chishti. The saint prophesied that the Emperor would be blessed with three sons. When Prince Salim (later Emperor Jahangir) was born, Akbar built his magnificent imperial capital here in gratitude.\n\nOccupied for only fourteen years before being abandoned due to water shortages in 1585, Fatehpur Sikri remains perfectly preserved. The Buland Darwaza (\"Gate of Magnificence\"), rising 54 meters, was built in 1601 to commemorate Akbar’s conquest of Gujarat, featuring an iconic inscription: \"The world is a bridge, pass over it, but build no house upon it.\"\n\nFatehpur Sikri invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Uttar Pradesh.",
    "loreTitle": "The Blessings of Salim Chishti & The Soaring Buland Darwaza",
    "ancientLegend": "Desperate for an heir, Emperor Akbar walked barefoot from Agra to the rocky ridge of Sikri to seek the blessings of the Sufi saint Sheikh Salim Chishti. The saint prophesied that the Emperor would be blessed with three sons. When Prince Salim (later Emperor Jahangir) was born, Akbar built his magnificent imperial capital here in gratitude.",
    "historicalChronicle": "Occupied for only fourteen years before being abandoned due to water shortages in 1585, Fatehpur Sikri remains perfectly preserved. The Buland Darwaza (\"Gate of Magnificence\"), rising 54 meters, was built in 1601 to commemorate Akbar’s conquest of Gujarat, featuring an iconic inscription: \"The world is a bridge, pass over it, but build no house upon it.\"",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Red Thread Wishes of Salim Chishti: Pilgrims tie red cotton threads to the intricately carved marble lattice screens (Jalis) of the saint’s tomb; when a heartfelt wish is fulfilled, devotees return to untie a thread.",
      "The Pachisi Court of Living Pawns: In the royal courtyard sits a giant stone board game of Pachisi, where Emperor Akbar and his courtiers played chess using slave girls in colorful dresses as living playing pieces."
    ],
