    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/04/Ahilya_Ghat_by_the_Ganges%2C_Varanasi.jpg/1280px-Ahilya_Ghat_by_the_Ganges%2C_Varanasi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "The spiritual capital of India and one of the oldest continuously inhabited cities in human history, Varanasi (Kashi/Benares) is the supreme sanctuary of Lord Shiva on the sacred northern bend of the Ganges.",
    "longDescription": "According to the Kashi Khanda, Varanasi is not situated on earthly ground, but rests on the tip of Lord Shiva’s cosmic trident (Trishula). To die in Kashi is to achieve instant Moksha (liberation from the cycle of rebirth), as Lord Shiva himself whispers the liberating Taraka mantra into the ear of the departing soul.\n\nWith 84 stone ghats stretching along the crescent curve of the Ganges, Varanasi has drawn seekers for three millennia, from Gautama Buddha (who preached his first sermon at nearby Sarnath) to Adi Shankaracharya, Kabir, and Tulsidas (who composed the Ramcharitmanas at Assi Ghat). The Kashi Vishwanath Jyotirlinga was gilded with one ton of gold by Maharaja Ranjit Singh in 1835.\n\nVaranasi invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Uttar Pradesh.",
    "loreTitle": "The Trident of Shiva & The Eternal Pyres of Manikarnika",
    "ancientLegend": "According to the Kashi Khanda, Varanasi is not situated on earthly ground, but rests on the tip of Lord Shiva’s cosmic trident (Trishula). To die in Kashi is to achieve instant Moksha (liberation from the cycle of rebirth), as Lord Shiva himself whispers the liberating Taraka mantra into the ear of the departing soul.",
    "historicalChronicle": "With 84 stone ghats stretching along the crescent curve of the Ganges, Varanasi has drawn seekers for three millennia, from Gautama Buddha (who preached his first sermon at nearby Sarnath) to Adi Shankaracharya, Kabir, and Tulsidas (who composed the Ramcharitmanas at Assi Ghat). The Kashi Vishwanath Jyotirlinga was gilded with one ton of gold by Maharaja Ranjit Singh in 1835.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The 3,000-Year-Old Eternal Fire of Manikarnika: The cremation fires at Manikarnika and Harishchandra Ghats have burned uninterruptedly 24 hours a day for thousands of years; legend says the flame was lit directly by Lord Shiva.",
      "The Ganga Aarti at Dashashwamedh: Every evening at twilight, seven young priests perform the synchronized Maha Aarti holding colossal multi-tiered brass lamps weighing 20 kilograms, accompanied by conch shells and Sanskrit hymns."
    ],
