    "id": "loc_0156",
    "name": "Badrinath",
    "tagline": "The high Himalayan sanctuary of the black stone Vishnu beneath the Neelkanth peak",
    "state": "Uttarakhand",
    "region": "Northern India",
    "category": "Spiritual",
    "secondaryCategories": [
      "Culture",
      "Heritage",
      "Nature"
    ],
    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f9/Badrinath_Temple_%2C_Uttarakhand.jpg/1280px-Badrinath_Temple_%2C_Uttarakhand.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "Perched at 10,279 feet between the Nar and Narayana mountain ranges beneath the pyramid peak of Neelkanth, Badrinath is the foremost of the four sacred Char Dham pilgrimages.",
    "longDescription": "According to the Vishnu Purana, Lord Vishnu performed intense penance in the freezing Himalayan blizzard. Goddess Lakshmi took the form of a wild berry tree (Badri or jujube tree) to shelter him from the snow and wind. Touched by her devotion, Vishnu named the sanctuary Badri-Nath (\"Lord of the Badri Tree\").\n\nIn the 8th century CE, the philosopher saint Adi Shankaracharya retrieved the sacred black Saligram stone idol of Lord Badrinarayan from the depths of the icy Alaknanda River and consecrated it inside the temple. The head priest (Rawal) is traditionally chosen from the Nambudiri Brahmin lineage of Kerala.\n\nBadrinath invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in Uttarakhand.",
    "loreTitle": "The Badri Tree of Parvati & The Tapt Kund Hot Spring",
    "ancientLegend": "According to the Vishnu Purana, Lord Vishnu performed intense penance in the freezing Himalayan blizzard. Goddess Lakshmi took the form of a wild berry tree (Badri or jujube tree) to shelter him from the snow and wind. Touched by her devotion, Vishnu named the sanctuary Badri-Nath (\"Lord of the Badri Tree\").",
    "historicalChronicle": "In the 8th century CE, the philosopher saint Adi Shankaracharya retrieved the sacred black Saligram stone idol of Lord Badrinarayan from the depths of the icy Alaknanda River and consecrated it inside the temple. The head priest (Rawal) is traditionally chosen from the Nambudiri Brahmin lineage of Kerala.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Boiling Water of Tapt Kund: Just below the temple terrace, natural hot sulphur springs bubble at 45°C despite freezing Himalayan snows; pilgrims bathe in the hot waters before entering the sanctum.",
      "The Last Village of Mana: Three kilometers beyond Badrinath lies Mana, the last Indian village before the Tibetan border, where Sage Vyasa composed the Mahabharata inside Vyas Gufa cave and Lord Ganesha broke his tusk to write the epic."
    ],
