    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9d/Rasmancha_Arnab_Dutta_2011.JPG/1280px-Rasmancha_Arnab_Dutta_2011.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "The 17th-century capital of the Malla kings, Bishnupur is world-famous for its magnificent terracotta temples, the Dalmadal cannon, and exquisite Baluchari silk sarees depicting epics.",
    "longDescription": "The Malla dynasty ruled Bishnupur for a thousand years. When King Bir Hambir converted to Gaudiya Vaishnavism under the saint Srinivasa Acharya, he transformed his warrior kingdom into a golden sanctuary of Radha-Krishna devotion. Because stone was scarce in the alluvial Bengal delta, royal artisans fired local alluvial clay into intricate terracotta tiles.\n\nThe Jor Bangla and Madan Mohan temples showcase dynamic terracotta panels depicting scenes from the Ramayana, Mahabharata, and rural Bengal life. The iconic 17th-century Rasmancha is a monumental pyramidal brick pavilion with concentric arched galleries where all village idols were gathered during the Raas festival.\n\nBishnupur invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in West Bengal.",
    "loreTitle": "The Terracotta Masterpieces of the Malla Dynasty & The Epic Baluchari Sarees",
    "ancientLegend": "The Malla dynasty ruled Bishnupur for a thousand years. When King Bir Hambir converted to Gaudiya Vaishnavism under the saint Srinivasa Acharya, he transformed his warrior kingdom into a golden sanctuary of Radha-Krishna devotion. Because stone was scarce in the alluvial Bengal delta, royal artisans fired local alluvial clay into intricate terracotta tiles.",
    "historicalChronicle": "The Jor Bangla and Madan Mohan temples showcase dynamic terracotta panels depicting scenes from the Ramayana, Mahabharata, and rural Bengal life. The iconic 17th-century Rasmancha is a monumental pyramidal brick pavilion with concentric arched galleries where all village idols were gathered during the Raas festival.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The Miracle of Dalmadal Cannon: The colossal 1742 forged-iron Dalmadal cannon is said to have been miraculously fired by Lord Madan Mohan (Krishna) himself in disguise to repel a Maratha Bargi invasion.",
      "The Baluchari Silk Mythological Weave: Master Bishnupur weavers weave complete narrative scenes from Hindu epics directly into the pallu of pure Baluchari silk sarees using jacquard looms."
    ],
