    "coverImage": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/72/Victoria_Memorial_situated_in_Kolkata.jpg/1280px-Victoria_Memorial_situated_in_Kolkata.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail",
    "description": "The cultural and intellectual soul of modern India, Kolkata is celebrated for its colonial Victorian marble monuments, yellow ambassador taxis, world-class Durga Puja art festivals, and literary heritage.",
    "longDescription": "The city takes its name from Kalighat, one of the 51 sacred Shaktipeeths where the four toes of Goddess Sati’s right foot fell into the sacred Adi Ganga canal. The 200-year-old Kalighat Kali Temple is the spiritual heart of Bengal’s tantric and devotional worship.\n\nAs the capital of British India until 1911, Kolkata was the cradle of the Bengal Renaissance, producing Nobel laureate Rabindranath Tagore, Swami Vivekananda, Satyajit Ray, and Subhas Chandra Bose. Iconic landmarks include the white marble Victoria Memorial, the cantilever Howrah Bridge (which carries 100,000 vehicles daily without any intermediate pillars in the river), and the bookstalls of College Street.\n\nKolkata invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in West Bengal.",
    "loreTitle": "The Goddess Kali of Kalighat & The Cultural Renaissance of Bengal",
    "ancientLegend": "The city takes its name from Kalighat, one of the 51 sacred Shaktipeeths where the four toes of Goddess Sati’s right foot fell into the sacred Adi Ganga canal. The 200-year-old Kalighat Kali Temple is the spiritual heart of Bengal’s tantric and devotional worship.",
    "historicalChronicle": "As the capital of British India until 1911, Kolkata was the cradle of the Bengal Renaissance, producing Nobel laureate Rabindranath Tagore, Swami Vivekananda, Satyajit Ray, and Subhas Chandra Bose. Iconic landmarks include the white marble Victoria Memorial, the cantilever Howrah Bridge (which carries 100,000 vehicles daily without any intermediate pillars in the river), and the bookstalls of College Street.",
    "folkloreTales": [
      "The UNESCO Durga Puja Spectacle: During Durga Puja in autumn, the entire city transforms into the world’s largest open-air contemporary art festival, with thousands of uniquely themed temporary bamboo and fabric temples (Pandals).",
      "The Flury’s and Indian Coffee House Debates: Kolkata’s legendary \"Adda\" (intellectual conversation) culture thrives in historic coffee houses where revolutionary political manifestos, avant-garde cinema, and poetry were debated over cups of black tea."
    ],
