    "description": "The world’s largest halophytic mangrove forest and delta, the Sundarbans is a UNESCO World Heritage wilderness of tidal waterways, mudflats, and the legendary swimming Royal Bengal tigers.",
    "longDescription": "Indigenous honey-gatherers (Mouleys) and fishermen deeply revere Goddess Bonbibi (\"The Lady of the Forest\"). According to the Bonbibi Johuranama folk epic, Bonbibi protects forest dwellers from the demon-tiger king Dokkhin Rai, provided humans take only what they need and never harm the balance of the mangrove wilderness.\n\nFormed at the confluence of the Ganges, Brahmaputra, and Meghna rivers across India and Bangladesh, the Sundarbans is named after the dense \"Sundari\" mangrove trees (Heritiera fomes) with their breathing roots (pneumatophores) rising from tidal mud. The Royal Bengal tigers here have uniquely adapted to drink saline water and swim across turbulent river channels.\n\nSundarbans invites wanderers to step into living chapters of sacred lore and timeless folklore in West Bengal.",
